const content = {
  "assets/storyboard-template.md": "# Storyboard / shot list — <project title>\n\n**Goal:** <what the viewer should feel/do>  **Platform:** <YouTube / Reels / ...>  **Length:** <N s>  **Aspect/fps:** <16:9 · 30>\n**Audio:** <music file, BPM, VO?>  **Look:** <grade preset / palette hex codes / font>  **Route:** <footage | parallax | Remotion | stylised live | animatic>\n\n| # | time (s) | shot / visual | motion & camera | text / graphics | audio / SFX | source file |\n|---|----------|---------------|-----------------|-----------------|-------------|-------------|\n| 1 | 0.0–2.0 | HOOK: <strongest frame> | push-in 1.0→1.10 | big title pop | riser starts | src/hook.mp4 |\n| 2 | 2.0–5.0 | <context> | static, slow drift | lower third | music in | |\n| 3 | 5.0–8.0 | <payoff / hit> | speed ramp → impact frame | — | impact + whoosh | |\n| 4 | 8.0–10.0 | <CTA / end card> | hold | logo + CTA | music out | |\n\n**Beat plan (music-led):** verse every 4 beats · pre-chorus every 2 · chorus every 1 · drop at <t> s\n**Risks / unknowns:** <missing assets, rights, fonts>\n**Approval:** draft preview → user feedback → final render\n",
  "assets/timeline.example.json": "{\n  \"output\": { \"path\": \"out/promo.mp4\", \"preset\": \"1080p30\", \"crf\": 18 },\n  \"defaults\": { \"fit\": \"cover\", \"transition\": { \"type\": \"cut\", \"duration\": 0 } },\n\n  \"clips\": [\n    { \"src\": \"src/hook.mp4\",   \"in\": 1.2, \"duration\": 2.0, \"zoom\": { \"from\": 1.0, \"to\": 1.10, \"ease\": \"out\" }, \"audio\": false },\n    { \"src\": \"src/scene2.mp4\", \"in\": 0.0, \"duration\": 3.0, \"audio\": false,\n      \"transition\": { \"type\": \"wipeleft\", \"duration\": 0.35 } },\n    { \"src\": \"src/impact.mp4\", \"in\": 0.5, \"duration\": 3.0, \"audio\": false,\n      \"ramp\": [[0, 1.0], [1.0, 1.0], [1.3, 0.25], [1.9, 1.0]] },\n    { \"image\": \"src/key_art.png\", \"duration\": 3.0, \"zoom\": { \"from\": 1.0, \"to\": 1.2, \"x\": [0.4, 0.6] },\n      \"transition\": { \"type\": \"fade\", \"duration\": 0.5 } },\n    { \"color\": \"black\", \"duration\": 1.0, \"transition\": { \"type\": \"fadeblack\", \"duration\": 0.5 } }\n  ],\n\n  \"grade\": \"cinematic\",\n\n  \"titles\": [\n    { \"text\": \"THE TITLE\", \"start\": 0.6, \"end\": 3.2, \"style\": \"center\", \"size\": 110, \"slide\": 40 },\n    { \"text\": \"A subtitle line\\nsecond line\", \"start\": 4.5, \"end\": 7.0, \"style\": \"lower_third\", \"size\": 44 }\n  ],\n\n  \"overlays\": [\n    { \"src\": \"src/logo.png\", \"x\": \"W-w-40\", \"y\": \"40\", \"width\": 140, \"opacity\": 0.9, \"fade_in\": 0.5 }\n  ],\n\n  \"subtitles\": \"work/titles.ass\",\n\n  \"audio\": {\n    \"music\": { \"src\": \"src/music.mp3\", \"volume\": 1.0, \"start\": 0.0, \"loop\": false, \"duck\": false, \"fade_out\": 2.0 }\n  },\n\n  \"fade_in\": 0.3,\n  \"fade_out\": 0.8,\n  \"loudnorm\": { \"lufs\": -14, \"tp\": -1.5 }\n}\n",
  "assets/titles.example.json": "{\n  \"width\": 1920,\n  \"height\": 1080,\n  \"font\": \"Poppins\",\n  \"bold\": true,\n  \"items\": [\n    { \"text\": \"POWER UP\", \"start\": 0.5, \"end\": 2.5, \"effect\": \"pop\", \"size\": 170, \"color\": \"#FFFFFF\", \"outline\": 6 },\n    { \"text\": \"this changes everything\", \"start\": 2.8, \"end\": 5.2, \"effect\": \"words\", \"size\": 120, \"pos\": \"bottom\" },\n    { \"text\": \"GLITCH\", \"start\": 5.5, \"end\": 7.0, \"effect\": \"glitch\", \"size\": 190 },\n    { \"text\": \"sing along with the highlight\", \"start\": 7.2, \"end\": 10.0, \"effect\": \"karaoke\", \"size\": 100, \"highlight\": \"#FFD400\", \"pos\": \"bottom\" }\n  ]\n}\n",
  "references/anime-production.md": "# Anime production — look, motion language, effects, and how to make anime video with whatever tools you have\n\nRead when the request mentions anime, AMV, manga/comic motion, sakuga, cel look, key art animation, lyric/music videos\nwith an anime aesthetic, or \"make it feel like an anime\".\nContents: what makes it \"anime\" · pipeline · capability discovery (what can I actually generate?) · build routes ·\nshot recipes · effects toolbox · colour & light · camera · sound · AMV editing · consistency · legal/ethical notes\n\n## What makes footage read as anime\n| trait | how it shows up | how to reproduce |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Limited animation / on twos & threes** | 12 or 8 distinct drawings per second inside a 24 fps timeline; holds are normal | `anime_fx.py --fx twos:n=2` (or `n=3`); keep camera moves smooth on ones for contrast |\n| **Held poses + selective fluidity** | most shots nearly still, a few beats (\"sakuga\") very fluid | spend motion budget on 1–2 hero moments per scene; hold the rest |\n| **Impact frames** | 1–3 frames of high-contrast ink/negative/white-out on a hit | `anime_fx.py --fx impact` / `--preset hit` |\n| **Smears & speed lines** | motion is drawn as distortion/lines, not blur | `anime_fx.py speedlines` plate + `blend:\"screen\"`; short `tmix` smear on fast pans |\n| **Camera shake & zoom punches** | on hits, explosions, stomps | `shake`, `punch` (decaying envelopes) |\n| **Flat colour + hard shadow shapes** | 2–3 tone cel shading, ink outlines | AI/hand art, or `cel` filter for live footage |\n| **Painted backgrounds + cel layer** | characters sit on detailed painted BG; BG often pans behind a static character | `parallax.py` with BG far, character near |\n| **Compositing (\"photography\") pass** | bloom/diffusion, soft vignette, chromatic fringing, film grain, lens flares, colour-graded sky | `glow`, `vignette`, `chroma`, `grain` — subtle (mix 0.25–0.45) |\n| **Exaggerated timing** | fast anticipation, snappy action, long settle | easeOutExpo + overshoot; see `motion-design.md` |\n| **Typography** | bold angular titles, vertical Japanese-style subtitles, impact text | `ass_titles.py` glitch/pop/shake_in |\n\n## Production pipeline (adapt to scope)\n1. **Concept & beat sheet** — logline, tone, length, platform. Music first if it is a music video (BPM/sections drive structure).\n2. **Storyboard / animatic** — one panel per shot with camera + action + duration (`assets/storyboard-template.md`).\n   Build the animatic early (stills + timing + rough audio) with `build_timeline.py`; fix pacing *before* making final art.\n3. **Assets** — character sheet (front/side/back, expressions, palette hex codes), background paintings, props, effect plates.\n4. **Key motion** — per shot choose a route (below); animate the hero moments first.\n5. **Compositing** — layers: BG → midground → character → FX (speed lines, particles) → lighting/glow → grade → grain.\n6. **Edit** — cut to the beat, add impact frames/flashes/shakes on hits, transitions only where motivated.\n7. **Sound** — music, SFX (whoosh, impact, cloth), ambience; duck music under dialogue.\n8. **QA & delivery** — `qa_check.py`, contact sheet, platform export (`delivery-specs.md`).\n\n## Capability discovery — decide the route from what exists\nRun `scripts/check_env.sh`, then look at your own tools: is there an image-generation tool/API, an image-to-video or\nvideo-generation tool/API, a text-to-speech tool, a Blender install, a GPU, ComfyUI/Stable-Diffusion tooling, or\nuser-supplied assets? **Do not promise what you cannot run.** State the route you will take and its limits in one sentence.\nIf web search is available and model/tool landscape matters (it changes fast), check current options before recommending.\nOptional helpers when installed or installable: Real-ESRGAN (anime upscaling), RIFE (frame interpolation), rembg\n(background removal for layer cut-outs), whisper/faster-whisper (captions), ComfyUI (node-based generation), Blender\n(3D/NPR toon shading), Manim (diagram animation). Install via pip/npm only if the network allowlist permits.\n\n## Build routes (best → most limited), pick per shot\n**A. User-supplied or tool-generated animated clips** (image-to-video / video generation, hand-drawn, 3D toon render)\n   → treat as footage: normalise (fps/res), `twos` if the clip is too smooth/AI-wobbly, `cel`/`glow`/`grain` pass for cohesion,\n   interpolate/upscale if needed, then edit. Generate 3–6 s clips per shot, not long takes (consistency drifts). Keep the same\n   character reference images, palette and seed/style prompt across all shots; reject shots where the face/outfit drifts.\n\n**B. 2.5D parallax from layered stills** (`parallax.py`) — reliable, controllable, works with nothing but images.\n   Needs layers: BG (opaque), midground (transparent PNG), character (transparent PNG), FX. Background removal (rembg) or\n   painting-by-layer produces cut-outs. Add `bob` on the character for breathing; push-in 4–8 %; light-leak/glow overlay.\n   This is the \"motion comic / animated key-art\" look and holds up well in music videos.\n\n**C. Programmatic motion graphics** (Remotion, SVG/Canvas, Pillow frames) — kinetic type, HUD/UI, diagrams, geometric\n   anime-style transitions (diagonal wipes, ink splashes, panel slides), lyric visualisers. Fully deterministic.\n\n**D. Stylise live footage** (`cel` + `glow` + `grain`, `twos`) — rotoscope-ish look from real video. Cheap, quality varies; works\n   best on high-contrast, well-lit footage; add outlines gently (`line` 0.08–0.14).\n\n**E. Animatic / motion-comic** — stills cut to music with push-ins, panel wipes, speed lines, impact frames. A legitimate\n   deliverable and the fastest path; set expectations that it is not full animation.\n\nSay which route(s) you used in the delivery note.\n\n## Shot recipes\n**Hit / strike (1.2 s)**: anticipation hold (0.4 s, slight zoom-out) → speed ramp to 1.0 → at contact: `impact` ink 2 frames →\nwhite flash 0.2 s → `shake` 0.5 s + `chroma` 0.25 s → radial `speedlines` plate for 0.6 s (on twos) → settle. Preset: `anime_fx.py apply … --preset hit --at T`.\n\n**Power-up / charge**: slow push-in (1.0 → 1.15), `glow` mix ramping up (use two segments), vibration (`boil`, small `shake` amp 4–6 continuously),\nrising audio riser; release with flash cut.\n\n**Dramatic still (hold + camera)**: 3–5 s hold, camera crawl 1.0 → 1.05, wind/hair loop or light flicker overlay, vignette + grain; text appears on the exhale.\n\n**Speed/dash**: parallel `speedlines` (`--style parallel`), BG motion-blurred with `tmix`, character smear frame (1–2 frames horizontally stretched) and a whoosh.\n\n**Establishing shot**: wide painted BG, slow `pan_right 0.05` parallax, birds/clouds layer moving at its own speed, title fade, ambient SFX.\n\n**Panel/manga transition**: diagonal `xfade` (`diagtl`, `wipetl`), or slice (`hlslice`), ≤ 0.35 s.\n\n**Flashback**: desaturate to ~60 %, warm/bloom, soft vignette, slightly lower contrast, 12 fps feel (`twos`), muffled low-pass audio.\n\n**Beat-flash montage**: cut every 1/2 beat in the chorus; 2-frame white or negative frame on each downbeat; 3–5 % zoom punch alternating.\n\n## Effects toolbox (scripts/anime_fx.py)\n`twos` `boil` `shake` `flash` `impact` `chroma` `glow` `grain` `vignette` `cel` `blur` `punch` `letterbox`, `speedlines` generator.\nPrinciples: effects are *accents* — 90 % of the video should be calm so the 10 % hits. Layer order: look (cel) → glow → hit effects → grain last.\nOverlay plates (speed lines, light leaks, sparkles): black background MP4 + timeline `overlay` with `\"blend\":\"screen\"`; transparent assets: ProRes 4444/WebM alpha with normal overlay.\n\n## Colour & light\n* Saturation up (1.2–1.45), contrast up a touch, blacks slightly lifted for the soft-painted feel (`curves=m='0/0.04 1/0.97'`).\n* **Time of day palettes**: dawn = peach/pink + lavender shadows; noon = cyan sky + warm highlights; golden hour = orange key + teal-purple shadows;\n  night = deep blue with magenta/cyan accent lights; interior = warm practicals vs cool windows.\n* Rim light + bloom on edges sells \"anime\" even on simple shading. Hard-edged shadow shapes; avoid smooth gradients on skin.\n* Use a **colour script**: one dominant hue per scene, accent complementary colour for the focus; keep a palette (5–7 hex codes) per project.\n* Grade presets in `build_timeline.py`: `anime_vivid`, `pastel`, `dreamy`, `cinematic`, `noir`; refine with `lut` or raw `grade.vf`.\n\n## Camera language\n* Anime \"camera\" = pans/tilts across a painted BG, track-in/out (zoom), and dutch on action. Avoid constant handheld.\n* Keep key shots on **ones or twos consistently** — mixing frame cadences within a shot looks like a bug.\n* Cut rhythm: dialogue 2–4 s; action 0.3–1.2 s; contemplative holds 4–6 s. Use match cuts on silhouettes/shapes.\n* Aspect: 16:9 default; 2.39:1 letterbox for cinematic/film-style; 9:16 for shorts (reframe — don't just crop).\n\n## Sound\nAnime relies on **SFX punctuation**: whooshes on camera moves, impact thumps on `impact` frames (+ 1-beat music dropout before), cloth/footstep foley,\nambience layers (wind, city, cicadas). Mix: dialogue/VO > SFX > music; duck music under speech; loudness −14 LUFS for online.\n\n## AMV / music-video editing\nFollow `editing-craft.md` → music-led edits; start from `autocut.py … --punch`, then hand-place: hero shots on chorus downbeats, speed ramps into drops,\nimpact frames on the biggest transients (`beat_detect.py` `onsets`), lyric text via `ass_titles.py` (`words`/`karaoke`).\nClip selection: match motion direction and energy across cuts; avoid reusing a moment; keep one grade across sources.\n\n## Consistency checklist (AI-assisted or multi-source work)\n- [ ] One character sheet; same outfit/colours/hairstyle across shots (compare thumbnails side by side via contact sheet)\n- [ ] Same resolution/fps/aspect for all shots before editing; same grade at the end\n- [ ] Hands/faces/text artefacts checked at full size on the shots that linger\n- [ ] Style cohesion pass (`glow` + `grain` + `vignette` over everything) to glue disparate clips\n\n## Rights & ethics\nDon't reproduce existing characters, logos or franchise artwork, and don't imitate a named living artist's work to pass it off as theirs. Original\ncharacters and generic \"anime style\" are fine. For music: user must hold rights or use licensed/royalty-free tracks for public release — mention it\nwhen delivering a music-led edit built on commercial songs. Don't use real people's likeness without permission.\n",
  "references/audio.md": "# Audio for video — loudness, music, ducking, cleanup, beat grids\n\nContents: targets · workflow · music selection & sync · beat grid verification · ducking · cleanup · SFX · common problems\n\n## Loudness targets (integrated LUFS / true peak)\n| destination | LUFS | true peak |\n|---|---|---|\n| YouTube, Instagram, TikTok, general streaming | **−14** | **−1.5 dBTP** (−1 is the spec; −1.5 leaves headroom for AAC re-encode) |\n| Podcast / voice-only | −16 (stereo) / −19 (mono) | −1.5 |\n| Broadcast (EBU R128) | −23 | −1.0 |\n| US broadcast (ATSC A/85) | −24 | −2.0 |\n| Cinema/trailers online | −16 to −18 | −1.5 |\nPlatforms normalise *down* if you are louder than their target and mostly leave quieter content alone, so don't squash dynamics chasing loudness.\nCheck with `scripts/loudnorm.py in.mp4 --measure-only` or `qa_check.py`.\n\n## Workflow\n1. Clean dialogue (high-pass 80 Hz, denoise if needed, gentle compression).\n2. Edit/cross-fade audio at cuts (5–30 ms fades to kill clicks; longer for ambience).\n3. Add music (duck under speech) and SFX.\n4. Master: limiter at −1 dBFS → two-pass loudnorm (`\"loudnorm\"` in the timeline, or `loudnorm.py`).\n5. Verify: `qa_check.py` (peaks, silence, loudness).\n\n## Music: choosing and syncing\n* Match mood and tempo to the cut rhythm: 90–110 BPM calm, 120–130 energetic, 140+ intense.\n* Pick the start offset deliberately (`audio.music.start`) — begin on a downbeat or a riser.\n* Ending: let the track resolve under the final image; fade the last 1.5–2 s (`fade_out`) unless the track ends cleanly on a hit.\n* Loops: `loop:true` repeats; use only for ambient beds. Cross-fade loop points if audible.\n* Licensing: user must have rights for public release; prefer royalty-free libraries when unsure.\n\n## Beat grid (cut-on-beat)\n```bash\npython3 scripts/beat_detect.py song.mp3 --out beats.json         # bpm, beats, downbeats, onsets\npython3 scripts/beat_detect.py song.mp3 --cuts 24 --min-gap 0.5   # N strong cut points\npython3 scripts/beat_detect.py song.mp3 --bpm 128 --offset 0.05   # correct a wrong tempo / constant offset\n```\nAccuracy: tested within ~20 ms on steady-tempo material. Weak spots: rubato/live tempo drift, ambient intros, half/double-time ambiguity\n(pass `--bpm` or `--bpm-range`). Beats assume a steady grid; for tempo-changing music, cut by `onsets` instead.\n**Verify before cutting a whole video to the grid** (an agent can't listen, so use numbers, and render clicks for the user/QA):\n```bash\npython3 scripts/click_track.py song.mp3 beats.json --report            # grid-vs-onset error in ms + drift check → GOOD / CHECK\npython3 scripts/click_track.py song.mp3 beats.json -o check_clicks.wav  # ticks on every beat (higher tick on downbeats) over the song\n```\nTested: correct grid → mean error ≈ 6 ms; deliberately wrong BPM → flagged CHECK (60 ms, only half the onsets match).\nCuts should land 1–2 frames *before* the beat to feel on-time (human perception forgives early). Subtract ~0.05 s from `beats`\nif the cut feels late.\n\n## Ducking (music under speech)\n`audio.music.duck:true` in the timeline (sidechain compress: threshold 0.04, ratio 9, attack 25 ms, release 450 ms).\nVoice-over supplied separately? Put it in the same timeline as clips' audio or mix manually:\n```bash\nffmpeg -i vo.wav -i music.mp3 -filter_complex \"[1:a][0:a]sidechaincompress=threshold=0.03:ratio=10:attack=20:release=400[m];[0:a][m]amix=inputs=2:duration=first:normalize=0\" mix.wav\n```\nManual keyframe ducking (precise): `volume='if(between(t,3,8),0.25,1)':eval=frame` (smooth with `afade` or a ramped expression).\nMusic level when speech plays: roughly 12–18 dB under the voice; check intelligibility on laptop speakers/phone, not just headphones.\n\n## Cleanup\n| problem | try |\n|---|---|\n| rumble, mic bumps | `highpass=f=80` |\n| hiss | `afftdn=nf=-25` (built-in) or `arnndn` with a model |\n| harsh sibilance | `equalizer=f=7000:t=q:w=2:g=-4` or a de-esser (`deesser`) |\n| boxy voice | `equalizer=f=300:t=q:w=1:g=-3` |\n| uneven level | `acompressor=threshold=-18dB:ratio=3:attack=10:release=200:makeup=3`, then loudnorm |\n| clicks/pops | `adeclick`, `adeclip` |\n| echo/room | hard; try `afftdn` + EQ; otherwise re-record |\n| dead air | `silenceremove` (see cookbook) |\n\n## SFX\nLayer small, short sounds: whoosh on camera/transitions (start ~3–5 frames before the visual lands), impact on hits (+ sub thump), UI ticks\non text pops, risers into drops. Keep SFX 6–12 dB under dialogue. Generate synthetic stand-ins if no library exists:\n```bash\nffmpeg -f lavfi -i \"anoisesrc=d=0.6:c=pink:a=0.5,highpass=f=300,lowpass=f=6000,afade=t=in:d=0.25,afade=t=out:st=0.25:d=0.35\" whoosh.wav\nffmpeg -f lavfi -i \"sine=f=55:d=0.35,afade=t=out:d=0.3,volume=1.5\" thump.wav\n```\n(Synthetic SFX are placeholders — tell the user to swap in real library sounds for a polished result.)\n\n## Common problems\n* **Audio longer/shorter than video** → check `qa_check.py av_sync`; use `-shortest` or pad with `apad` + `-t`.\n* **Pops at cuts** → add 10 ms `afade` in/out per clip, or crossfade.\n* **Loudness lower than target** → peak-limited; compress/limit earlier, or accept (don't clip).\n* **Mono source in stereo project** → `aformat=channel_layouts=stereo` (done in timeline) duplicates to both channels.\n* **Sample-rate mismatch** → always `aresample=48000` (done in timeline).\n",
  "references/color.md": "# Colour — grading, matching, colour spaces, HDR → SDR\n\nContents: golden rules · ffmpeg grading toolkit · looks · matching shots · LUTs · colour-space hygiene · HDR → SDR · checks\n\n## Golden rules\n1. **Fix before you stylise**: exposure → white balance → contrast → saturation → *then* the look.\n2. **One grade per video** applied consistently (global `grade`), with per-clip corrections only to *match* shots.\n3. Work in Rec.709 SDR for web delivery; tag the output (`-colorspace bt709 -color_primaries bt709 -color_trc bt709`).\n4. Check skin tones and neutral greys first; check the contact sheet side by side; step back — subtle beats heavy.\n5. Don't crush: keep detail in blacks/whites unless the look demands it. Use a waveform (`waveform` filter) to see clipping.\n\n## ffmpeg grading toolkit\n```\neq=brightness=0.03:contrast=1.08:saturation=1.15:gamma=0.98        # basic; brightness −1..1 (small steps), gamma 0.1..10\ncolorbalance=rs=-0.05:bs=0.06:rm=0.02:bm=-0.02:rh=0.06:bh=-0.05     # shadows/mids/highlights R/G/B shifts (teal-orange)\ncolortemperature=temperature=5200                                     # white balance in Kelvin (ffmpeg ≥ 5.1)\ncurves=m='0/0.04 0.5/0.5 1/0.96'  | curves=preset=cross_process       # contrast / lifted blacks / film-like curves; r= g= b= for channels\ncurves=all='0/0 0.25/0.2 0.75/0.82 1/1'                               # gentle S-curve\nhue=h=10:s=1.2                                                         # rotate hue (degrees), saturation\nselectivecolor=reds=0 0 0 0:…                                          # per-colour-range CMYK-style adjust\ncolorchannelmixer=…                                                    # matrix mixing (B&W with custom weights, tints)\nlut3d=file=look.cube                                                   # apply a 3D LUT (.cube/.3dl)\nvignette=angle=PI/5   noise=alls=8:allf=t+u   unsharp=5:5:0.8:3:3:0.0   gblur=sigma=0.8\n```\nPreview a grade on one frame fast: `ffmpeg -ss 5 -i in.mp4 -frames:v 1 -vf \"<filters>\" test.png` and look at it.\n\n## Looks (starting points; presets exist in build_timeline.py)\n| look | recipe idea |\n|---|---|\n| cinematic teal-orange | lift blue in shadows, warm highlights, contrast 1.1, slight vignette, blacks lifted to ~0.02 |\n| anime vivid | saturation 1.3–1.5, contrast 1.1, light unsharp, small bloom (`glow`) |\n| pastel / soft | contrast 0.92, brightness +0.05, lifted blacks 0.08, saturation 1.1 |\n| noir | `hue=s=0`, contrast 1.3–1.4, vignette strong |\n| warm nostalgic | `colorbalance` +R/−B in mids, blacks lifted, grain (alls 6–8), saturation 0.92 |\n| cold thriller | −R/+B mids, desaturate 0.85, contrast 1.15, crushed blacks |\n| day-for-night | exposure −1 stop (`eq=brightness=-0.25`), blue tint, contrast up, vignette; fake only wide shots |\n\n## Matching shots (the invisible craft)\n* Pick a **hero shot** and match others to it: compare mean luma and white balance (`signalstats` filter prints YAVG/UAVG/VAVG):\n  `ffmpeg -i clip.mp4 -vf \"signalstats,metadata=print:file=-\" -f null - | grep -E \"YAVG|UAVG|VAVG\" | head`\n* Nudge exposure with `eq=brightness`, balance with `colorbalance`/`colortemperature` per clip (`clip.grade` object with `vf`).\n* Auto-levels as a first pass on dull footage: `normalize=blackpt=black:whitept=white:smoothing=30` (temporal smoothing avoids flicker).\n\n## LUTs\n`lut3d=file=look.cube` — apply *after* exposure/white-balance fixes and match the LUT's expected input space (log LUTs expect log footage;\nusing one on Rec.709 footage looks wrong). Blend strength: split + `blend=all_mode=normal:all_opacity=0.6`. Timeline: `\"grade\": \"looks/film.cube\"`.\n\n## Colour-space hygiene\n* Most phone/web footage is already BT.709 SDR; nothing to convert. Tag the output anyway.\n* RGB sources (PNG/Remotion/Pillow) → YUV: use `-vf scale=out_color_matrix=bt709:flags=lanczos,format=yuv420p` to avoid washed/saturated mismatches;\n  add `-colorspace bt709 -color_primaries bt709 -color_trc bt709 -color_range tv`.\n* Washed-out or too contrasty on some players = range/matrix mismatch: check `ffprobe` color_range (tv vs pc/jpeg) and color_space.\n* `yuvj420p` (full-range JPEG-style) → convert: `-vf scale=in_range=pc:out_range=tv,format=yuv420p`.\n* 10-bit sources: `format=yuv420p` for delivery (dither: `scale=…:flags=…+accurate_rnd+full_chroma_int` when banding appears; add light `noise`/`deband`).\n\n## HDR → SDR (iPhone HLG/Dolby Vision, PQ footage)\nDetect: `probe_media.py` flags `color_transfer` smpte2084 (PQ) / arib-std-b67 (HLG).\n```bash\nffmpeg -i hdr.mov -vf \"zscale=t=linear:npl=100,format=gbrpf32le,zscale=p=bt709,tonemap=tonemap=hable:desat=0,zscale=t=bt709:m=bt709:r=tv,format=yuv420p\" \\\n       -c:v libx264 -crf 18 -colorspace bt709 -color_primaries bt709 -color_trc bt709 sdr.mp4\n```\nIf ffmpeg answers `no path between colorspaces`, the file carries no colour tags; declare them in front of the chain\n(tested): `setparams=color_primaries=bt2020:color_trc=smpte2084:colorspace=bt2020nc:range=tv,zscale=t=linear:npl=100,…`\n(HLG: `color_trc=arib-std-b67`).\nNeeds `zscale` (libzimg) — `check_env.sh` reports it. Without it, use `scale` + `colorspace` filter (lower quality) or ask the user for SDR export.\nMixing HDR and SDR clips without tone-mapping gives dull/blown-out clips.\n\n## Checks\n* Waveform: `ffmpeg -i in.mp4 -vf \"waveform=filter=lowpass:scale=digital\" -frames:v 1 wf.png`\n* Vectorscope for skin line: `-vf \"vectorscope=mode=color2\"`\n* Contact sheet before/after at the same timestamps; view at thumbnail size to judge the overall palette.\n",
  "references/delivery-specs.md": "# Delivery specs — formats, platforms, export presets\n\n**Platform limits and recommendations change often.** The values below are safe, conservative defaults. If the exact\nlimit matters (max length, file size, new aspect support) and web search is available, check the platform's current\nhelp page before promising. When in doubt: H.264 High + AAC in MP4, yuv420p, `+faststart`.\n\nContents: universal master · platform table · safe zones · aspect-ratio conversions · export commands · deliverables checklist\n\n## Universal \"plays everywhere\" file\n```bash\nffmpeg -i master.mov -c:v libx264 -preset slow -crf 18 -pix_fmt yuv420p -profile:v high -level 4.2 \\\n  -colorspace bt709 -color_primaries bt709 -color_trc bt709 \\\n  -c:a aac -b:a 192k -ar 48000 -movflags +faststart final.mp4\n```\n* CFR (constant frame rate), even dimensions, square pixels, yuv420p, 48 kHz stereo AAC, `moov` atom at the front (`faststart`).\n* Keep a high-quality master (ProRes 422 HQ or CRF 14 H.264) so you can re-export for other platforms without generation loss.\n\n## Platform cheat-sheet (verify when it matters)\n| destination | aspect / size | fps | video | audio | notes |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| YouTube (landscape) | 16:9; 1080p or 4K | match source (24/30/60) | H.264 (or VP9/AV1 re-encoded by YT) 8–12 Mbps @1080p30, 35–45 Mbps @4K | AAC 48 kHz, −14 LUFS | upload highest quality; YT re-encodes |\n| YouTube Shorts | 9:16, 1080×1920 | 30/60 | H.264 | AAC | length limit exists (currently up to ~3 min; check) |\n| Instagram Reels | 9:16, 1080×1920 | 30 | H.264, ≤ ~10 Mbps | AAC 48 kHz | respect UI safe zones; length limit changes |\n| TikTok | 9:16, 1080×1920 | 30 | H.264/H.265 | AAC | similar safe zones; captions/text inside safe area |\n| Instagram feed / Facebook | 4:5 (1080×1350) or 1:1 (1080×1080) or 16:9 | 30 | H.264 | AAC | 4:5 gets most screen space in feed |\n| X / LinkedIn | 16:9 or 1:1 (or 9:16) | 30 | H.264 | AAC | check file-size/length caps |\n| Web embed / site hero | 16:9; also provide WebM/AV1 + poster | 24–30 | H.264 MP4 + VP9/AV1 WebM | muted autoplay: omit audio or `-an` | keep < ~5–8 MB for hero loops |\n| Presentation / PowerPoint | 16:9 1080p | 30 | H.264 MP4 | AAC | avoid exotic codecs; embed test |\n| Broadcast / festival | per spec sheet | 23.976/25/29.97 | ProRes 422 HQ / DNxHR, 10-bit | PCM 48 kHz −23 LUFS | ask for the spec document |\n| Transparent overlay | any | match project | ProRes 4444 (yuva444p10le) .mov or VP9 alpha .webm | — | pro editors accept ProRes 4444 |\n| GIF / loop | ≤ 720 px wide | 12–15 | palette GIF or animated WebP | none | WebP/MP4 loops are smaller and cleaner |\n\n## Safe zones for vertical video (1080×1920)\nKeep essential text/faces inside roughly: **top 14 % free, bottom 25 % free, sides ≥ 6 %** (UI overlays: username, caption, buttons).\nPut captions at ~65–72 % of the height. Verify with a mock overlay on the contact sheet if the layout is tight.\n\n## Converting aspect ratios\n* Prefer **re-framing** per shot (`zoom.x/y` focus, `fit: cover`) over blind centre crops; use `fit: blurpad` only for content that must stay fully visible.\n* Re-layout text for the new aspect (don't just scale); in Remotion size from `width/height`.\n* Provide each aspect as a separate export from the same timeline: change `output.preset` (`1080p30` → `vertical` → `square`).\n\n## Export commands\n```bash\n# Timeline → multiple deliverables (same edit, different canvas; re-frame per shot afterwards if needed)\nfor P in 1080p30 vertical square; do\n  python3 scripts/build_timeline.py timeline.json --preset $P -o out/final_$P.mp4\ndone\n# Archive master\nffmpeg -i final.mp4 -c:v prores_ks -profile:v 3 -pix_fmt yuv422p10le -c:a pcm_s16le master.mov\n# Small preview for chat/email (≈ 3–6 MB per 10 s at 720p)\nffmpeg -i final.mp4 -vf scale=-2:720 -c:v libx264 -crf 26 -preset veryfast -c:a aac -b:a 96k preview_720p.mp4\n# Poster frame\nffmpeg -ss 3 -i final.mp4 -frames:v 1 -q:v 2 poster.jpg\n# Target a file size (e.g. 25 MB for 60 s incl. audio): video kbps ≈ (25*8192/60) − 128 ≈ 3290 → two-pass\nffmpeg -y -i in.mp4 -c:v libx264 -b:v 3290k -pass 1 -an -f null /dev/null && \\\nffmpeg -i in.mp4 -c:v libx264 -b:v 3290k -pass 2 -c:a aac -b:a 128k out.mp4\n```\n`--preset` overrides `output.preset` (and drops fixed width/height). Landscape→vertical usually needs per-shot reframing (`zoom.x/y`, `fit`) and re-laid-out titles: use a copy of the timeline for the hero deliverable.\n\n## Deliverables checklist\n- [ ] Exact format asked for (container, codec, resolution, fps, aspect, length)\n- [ ] `qa_check.py` with `--expect-*` flags → PASS (or WARN explained)\n- [ ] Looked at the contact sheet (first frame, last frame, every transition, every title)\n- [ ] File opens in a normal player (yuv420p, faststart); size reasonable for where it's going\n- [ ] Optional extras the user probably wants: poster frame, vertical/square cut, captions file (.srt), project folder with timeline JSON so changes are cheap\n",
  "references/editing-craft.md": "# Editing craft — decisions that make an edit feel professional\n\nTechnical correctness is table stakes; this file is about *taste*. Read it when planning an edit, choosing\npacing, or reviewing a cut that \"works but feels off\".\n\nContents: the planning questions · structure · pacing · cutting rules · music-led edits · social/vertical ·\ntext on screen · sound · review checklist\n\n## Before touching footage: five questions\n1. **Purpose** — what should the viewer think/feel/do at the end? (Sell, explain, move, entertain.)\n2. **Audience & platform** — decides aspect ratio, length, pace, caption need (see `delivery-specs.md`).\n3. **Length budget** — pick a number first (e.g. 30 s). Every shot must earn its seconds.\n4. **Hook & payoff** — what is the first 2 seconds? what is the last image people remember?\n5. **Assets in hand** — footage, stills, music, voice-over, brand rules (fonts, colours, logo). Probe everything.\n\nWrite a 6–12 line shot list / storyboard (`assets/storyboard-template.md`) before building. Cheap to change, expensive later.\n\n## Structure templates\n| format | skeleton |\n|---|---|\n| 15–30 s social ad | hook (0–2 s) → problem/curiosity → payoff/product → CTA (last 3 s) |\n| 60–90 s explainer | cold open → context → 3 beats of \"claim + visual proof\" → recap → CTA |\n| trailer / hype | slow build → rising tempo cuts → silence-drop → hit montage → title card |\n| AMV / music montage | intro (hold) → verse (medium pace, story) → pre-chorus (accelerate) → chorus (fastest, best shots, flashes) → outro |\n| vlog / interview | strongest line first → context → story with b-roll covering jump cuts → button |\n| product demo | outcome first → 3 features, each 5–8 s, UI zooms → social proof → CTA |\n\n## Pacing (numbers to start from, then adjust by feel)\n* Average shot length: calm 4–8 s · dialogue 3–6 s · energetic social 1–2.5 s · action/AMV chorus 0.3–1 s.\n* Vary it. Monotone cutting numbs; accelerate into peaks, hold after them.\n* A shot needs enough time to be *read*: ≥ ~1 s for a new scene, ≥ 2 s if it carries text.\n* On-screen text: ~3 words/second reading speed; hold 1 s extra. Titles ≥ 1.5 s even if short.\n* Cut dead air: remove breaths/ums/pauses > 0.4 s in talking-head; keep deliberate silence for drama only.\n\n## Cutting rules (and when to break them)\n* **Cut on action**: switch angle mid-movement; motion hides the seam.\n* **Match cut**: same shape/position/motion across the cut (door → door, wheel → clock). Strong transitions without effects.\n* **Don't jump-cut similar framings** (same size, same angle) unless it's a deliberate jump-cut style; change size ≥ 30% or angle ≥ 30°.\n* **Shot variety**: wide (where), medium (who), close (feel), insert/cutaway (detail). Sequence rhythm: W–M–C–insert.\n* **Screen direction**: keep subjects moving the same direction across cuts unless showing a reversal (180° rule).\n* **J-cut / L-cut**: let audio lead (J) or trail (L) the picture by 0.3–1 s; the single biggest \"smooth\" trick for dialogue. In build_timeline: handle by placing voice-over/music as an audio track independent of clips (clips `audio:false`).\n* **Cut on the beat** for music-led edits, ± 1–2 frames *early* (the eye forgives early, not late): shift cut ~2 frames before the beat.\n* **Transitions**: 80%+ hard cuts. A transition must mean something (time jump = dissolve, chapter = fade through black).\n* **Reaction/hold**: after the big moment, hold one extra beat so it lands.\n* **Rule of thirds / headroom / lead room**: crop with `fit:\"cover\"` and a zoom focus (`zoom.x/y`) to re-frame subjects on thirds.\n\n## Music-led edits\n1. `beat_detect.py` → BPM + beat grid; verify (click track, see `audio.md`).\n2. Choose cut density per section: verse every 4 beats (1 bar), pre-chorus every 2, chorus every 1 (or 1/2).\n3. Put the *best* footage on the downbeats of the chorus/drop. Put a flash/impact (`anime_fx.py --preset hit`) on the biggest hit.\n4. Hide repetition: never reuse the same 2 seconds of source; rotate sources (`autocut.py` does this).\n5. Add motion variety: alternating push-in/pull-out (`--punch`), speed ramps into drops, a 1-beat freeze before the drop.\n6. Drop the music out for 1–2 beats before the final hit — contrast creates impact.\n\n## Social / vertical (9:16)\n* Hook in ≤ 2 s: movement + big text or a surprising frame. No logo intros.\n* Safe zones: keep key content out of the top ~14 % and bottom ~25 % (UI overlays) and ~6 % of the sides.\n* Captions on by default (most watch muted): 2 lines max, high contrast, word-pop style engages. Size ≥ 6 % of frame height.\n* Pace ≥ 1 visual change per 1.5–3 s (cut, zoom, text pop, B-roll).\n* End on a loopable frame or a clear CTA.\n\n## Text on screen\n* Contrast first: white + 2–3 px dark outline/shadow or a semi-opaque box beats any fancy colour.\n* 1–2 typefaces max; bold sans for headlines; consistent position per text type (lower-thirds always same place).\n* Entrance ≈ 0.25–0.4 s ease-out; exit ≈ 0.2 s ease-in; don't animate text while the viewer must read it.\n* Keep inside title-safe (≥ 5 % margin). Check on the contact sheet at thumbnail size: if unreadable there, it's too small.\n\n## Sound is half the picture\n* Dialogue/VO sits at about −16 LUFS-equivalent loudness; music 12–18 dB under speech while speaking (duck), full level in gaps.\n* Add room tone/ambience under cuts so silence isn't digital-black; add small SFX (whooshes on transitions, hits on impacts) — mixed low.\n* Fade audio at the very start/end (5–30 ms) to avoid clicks; finish with the loudness pass.\n\n## Review checklist (run before delivering)\n- [ ] Hook works in the first 2 s (look at frames 0–60 on the contact sheet)\n- [ ] No unintended black/frozen frames, no flash frames, no duplicated shots\n- [ ] Text legible at thumbnail size, inside safe margins, no typos (read every string)\n- [ ] Audio: no clipping, speech intelligible over music, consistent loudness, no dead air\n- [ ] Colour consistent across shots (one grade, matched exposure)\n- [ ] Duration & format match the brief; `qa_check.py` is PASS/understood WARN\n- [ ] Ending lands (hold, fade, CTA, loop)\n",
  "references/ffmpeg-cookbook.md": "# ffmpeg cookbook — recipes you will actually need\n\nUse `build_timeline.py` for assembly; use these for one-off operations, repairs and building blocks.\nContents: trimming · concat · normalising inputs · scaling/vertical · speed · transitions · overlays/PiP/split ·\ntext · alpha & keying · stabilise · audio · GIF/WebP · image sequences · encoding choices · analysis · visualisers\n\nConventions: `-y` overwrite; add `-hide_banner -loglevel warning -stats` to keep logs short. Always quote paths.\nFilter expressions containing commas must be single-quoted: `enable='between(t,1,3)'`.\n\n## Trimming\n```bash\n# Frame-accurate (re-encodes). -ss BEFORE -i is fast AND accurate when re-encoding.\nffmpeg -ss 00:00:12.5 -i in.mp4 -t 4 -c:v libx264 -crf 18 -c:a aac out.mp4\n# Lossless & instant, but snaps to keyframes (start can be off by up to a GOP):\nffmpeg -ss 12.5 -to 16.5 -i in.mp4 -c copy -avoid_negative_ts make_zero out.mp4\n```\n\n## Concatenate\n```bash\n# Same codec/params, no re-encode (list.txt lines: file 'a.mp4'):\nffmpeg -f concat -safe 0 -i list.txt -c copy out.mp4\n# Different sources → re-encode via filter (normalise first!). Prefer build_timeline.py.\nffmpeg -i a.mp4 -i b.mp4 -filter_complex \"[0:v][0:a][1:v][1:a]concat=n=2:v=1:a=1[v][a]\" -map \"[v]\" -map \"[a]\" out.mp4\n```\n\n## Normalise messy inputs (do this when anything misbehaves)\n```bash\n# Variable frame rate (phone/screen recordings) → constant, plus even dims, yuv420p, 48k stereo\nffmpeg -i in.mov -vf \"fps=30,scale=trunc(iw/2)*2:trunc(ih/2)*2,format=yuv420p\" -c:v libx264 -crf 18 \\\n       -c:a aac -ar 48000 -ac 2 norm.mp4\n# Bake rotation metadata into pixels: ffmpeg autorotates by default; to keep manual control add -noautorotate\n# Non-square pixels: append ,setsar=1 after scaling\n```\n\n## Scaling, cropping, vertical/horizontal conversion\n```bash\n# 16:9 → 9:16 centre crop (loses sides)\nffmpeg -i in.mp4 -vf \"crop=ih*9/16:ih,scale=1080:1920\" out.mp4\n# 16:9 → 9:16 keep everything: sharp video over blurred copy of itself\nffmpeg -i in.mp4 -filter_complex \"[0:v]split[a][b];[a]scale=1080:1920:force_original_aspect_ratio=increase,crop=1080:1920,gblur=sigma=40[bg];[b]scale=1080:-2[fg];[bg][fg]overlay=(W-w)/2:(H-h)/2\" out.mp4\n# Fit inside 1920x1080 with black bars\n-vf \"scale=1920:1080:force_original_aspect_ratio=decrease,pad=1920:1080:(ow-iw)/2:(oh-ih)/2\"\n# Fill 1920x1080 by cropping the excess\n-vf \"scale=1920:1080:force_original_aspect_ratio=increase,crop=1920:1080\"\n# Scope letterbox 2.39:1 on 1920x1080\n-vf \"crop=1920:804,pad=1920:1080:0:138:black\"\n# Upscale decently\n-vf \"scale=3840:2160:flags=lanczos\"       # ML upscalers (Real-ESRGAN) beat this for anime stills/frames\n```\n\n## Speed\n```bash\nffmpeg -i in.mp4 -filter_complex \"[0:v]setpts=0.5*PTS[v];[0:a]atempo=2.0[a]\" -map \"[v]\" -map \"[a]\" fast.mp4   # 2×\nffmpeg -i in.mp4 -filter_complex \"[0:v]setpts=2.0*PTS[v];[0:a]atempo=0.5[a]\" -map \"[v]\" -map \"[a]\" slow.mp4   # 0.5×\n# Smooth slow-mo by synthesising frames (slow; blend is safer than mci for anime/line art)\n-vf \"setpts=4*PTS,minterpolate=fps=30:mi_mode=blend\"\n# atempo accepts 0.5–2.0 per instance: chain for more (atempo=2.0,atempo=2.0 = 4×)\n# Reverse (loads whole clip in RAM — keep clips short): -vf reverse -af areverse\n# Freeze a frame for 2 s at t=3: grab the frame, then use it as an image clip in the timeline\n#   ffmpeg -ss 3 -i in.mp4 -frames:v 1 freeze.png   →  {\"image\":\"freeze.png\",\"duration\":2}  (place between the two halves of the clip)\n```\n\n## Fades and transitions\n```bash\n-vf \"fade=t=in:st=0:d=1,fade=t=out:st=9:d=1\" -af \"afade=t=in:d=1,afade=t=out:st=9:d=1\"\n# xfade needs identical size/fps/pixfmt/timebase. offset = (len of first) − (transition duration)\nffmpeg -i a.mp4 -i b.mp4 -filter_complex \"[0:v][1:v]xfade=transition=fade:duration=1:offset=4[v];[0:a][1:a]acrossfade=d=1[a]\" -map \"[v]\" -map \"[a]\" out.mp4\n# list transitions this build supports: ffmpeg -h filter=xfade\n```\nCustom transition: `xfade=transition=custom:expr='...'` (per-pixel expression with A, B, P, X, Y, W, H, PLANE).\n\n## Overlays, picture-in-picture, split screen\n```bash\n# Logo top-right, 30 px margin, 80% opacity\n-filter_complex \"[1:v]format=rgba,colorchannelmixer=aa=0.8[l];[0:v][l]overlay=W-w-30:30\"\n# PiP bottom-right at 25% width, visible only 2–8 s\n-filter_complex \"[1:v]scale=iw*0.25:-1[p];[0:v][p]overlay=W-w-40:H-h-40:enable='between(t,2,8)'\"\n# Side by side / grid\n-filter_complex \"[0:v][1:v]hstack=inputs=2\"       |      \"[0:v][1:v][2:v][3:v]xstack=inputs=4:layout=0_0|w0_0|0_h0|w0_h0\"\n# Blend modes for plates on black (glow, light leaks, speed lines): blend=all_mode=screen\n```\n\n## Text\n```bash\n# drawtext with fade + shadow; use textfile= to dodge escaping\n-vf \"drawtext=fontfile=/path/Font.ttf:textfile=t.txt:expansion=none:fontsize=72:fontcolor=white:x=(w-text_w)/2:y=h*0.8:shadowx=2:shadowy=2:alpha='min(1,(t-1)/0.4)':enable='between(t,1,4)'\"\n# Burn SRT / ASS\n-vf \"subtitles=captions.srt:force_style='FontName=Poppins,FontSize=26,Outline=2,MarginV=60'\"      -vf \"ass=titles.ass\"\n# Font lookup: fc-list : family | sort -u ; pass fontsdir=/path to ass/subtitles for bundled fonts\n```\nAuto-captions: `whisper audio.mp3 --model small --output_format srt` (openai-whisper) or faster-whisper; fix names/jargon by hand;\nfor word-pop captions feed word timestamps (`--word_timestamps True`) into `ass_titles.py` (`words` effect).\n\n## Alpha, keying, masks\n```bash\n# Green screen\n-filter_complex \"[1:v]chromakey=0x00ff00:0.12:0.08,despill=type=green[fg];[0:v][fg]overlay\"\n# Export with transparency\nffmpeg -i in.mp4 ... -c:v prores_ks -profile:v 4444 -pix_fmt yuva444p10le out.mov     # editing/compositing\nffmpeg -i in.mp4 ... -c:v libvpx-vp9 -pix_fmt yuva420p out.webm                          # web\n# Image sequence with alpha → video: -framerate 24 -i f_%04d.png (PNG keeps alpha)\n```\n\n## Stabilise (two-pass)\n```bash\nffmpeg -i shaky.mp4 -vf vidstabdetect=shakiness=6:result=t.trf -f null -\nffmpeg -i shaky.mp4 -vf \"vidstabtransform=input=t.trf:smoothing=15:zoom=2,unsharp=5:5:0.8\" -c:a copy steady.mp4\n```\n\n## Audio\n```bash\n-af \"volume=0.5\"   -af \"volume=-6dB\"\n-af \"highpass=f=80,lowpass=f=14000\"                                  # clean rumble/hiss\n-af \"acompressor=threshold=-18dB:ratio=3:attack=10:release=200:makeup=3\"   # even out speech\n-af \"equalizer=f=3000:t=q:w=1:g=3\"                                    # presence\n-af \"silenceremove=start_periods=1:start_threshold=-45dB:stop_periods=-1:stop_duration=0.6:stop_threshold=-45dB\"  # cut dead air\n-af \"adelay=1500|1500\"                                                # delay 1.5 s (stereo)\n-af \"arnndn=m=model.rnnn\"                                            # RNN denoise (needs a model file)\n-af \"afftdn=nf=-25\"                                                   # built-in denoiser\n# Duck music under voice (voice.wav = sidechain)\n-filter_complex \"[1:a][0:a]sidechaincompress=threshold=0.04:ratio=9:attack=25:release=450[m];[0:a][m]amix=inputs=2:duration=first:normalize=0\"\n# Replace/mux audio, keep video untouched\nffmpeg -i video.mp4 -i music.mp3 -map 0:v -map 1:a -c:v copy -c:a aac -shortest out.mp4\n# Extract audio\nffmpeg -i in.mp4 -vn -c:a pcm_s16le audio.wav\n```\nLoudness: `scripts/loudnorm.py`. Details in `audio.md`.\n\n## GIF / WebP / images\n```bash\n# Good GIF (palette), 12 fps, 640 wide\nffmpeg -i in.mp4 -vf \"fps=12,scale=640:-1:flags=lanczos,split[a][b];[a]palettegen=stats_mode=diff[p];[b][p]paletteuse=dither=bayer:bayer_scale=4\" out.gif\nffmpeg -i in.mp4 -vf \"fps=15,scale=720:-1\" -c:v libwebp_anim -loop 0 -q:v 70 out.webp     # smaller & better than GIF\nffmpeg -ss 5 -i in.mp4 -frames:v 1 -q:v 2 thumb.jpg                                          # single frame\nffmpeg -i in.mp4 -vf \"fps=1,scale=320:-1\" thumbs/%04d.png                                    # 1 per second\nffmpeg -framerate 24 -i frames/f_%04d.png -c:v libx264 -pix_fmt yuv420p -crf 16 out.mp4      # image sequence → video\n```\n\n## Encoding choices\n| goal | flags |\n|---|---|\n| universal web/social | `-c:v libx264 -preset medium -crf 18 -pix_fmt yuv420p -profile:v high -movflags +faststart -c:a aac -b:a 192k` |\n| smaller, modern | `-c:v libx265 -crf 23 -tag:v hvc1 -pix_fmt yuv420p` (Apple needs `hvc1`) |\n| archive/edit master | `-c:v prores_ks -profile:v 3 -pix_fmt yuv422p10le -c:a pcm_s16le` (.mov) |\n| web, open codec | `-c:v libvpx-vp9 -crf 30 -b:v 0 -c:a libopus` (.webm); AV1: `libsvtav1 -crf 32` |\n| fast draft | `-preset ultrafast -crf 30` and half resolution |\n| target file size | two-pass `-b:v` (bitrate = size_bits / duration − audio) |\n| GPU | `h264_nvenc -cq 19`, `h264_videotoolbox -q:v 60`, `h264_vaapi` — quicker, slightly larger/lower quality; confirm hardware exists first |\nCRF scale: 14–16 near-lossless, 18 high quality, 23 default, 28 low. Animation/flat colour: add `-tune animation`;\ngrainy film: `-tune grain`. Always `-pix_fmt yuv420p` for delivery, `-movflags +faststart` for MP4s on the web.\n\n## Analysis & debugging\n```bash\nffprobe -v error -show_format -show_streams -of json in.mp4         # everything (or scripts/probe_media.py)\nffmpeg -i in.mp4 -vf \"blackdetect=d=0.1,freezedetect=n=0.003:d=0.5\" -an -f null -     # black/frozen frames\nffmpeg -i in.mp4 -af \"silencedetect=n=-50dB:d=1,ebur128=peak=true\" -vn -f null -        # silence + loudness\nffmpeg -i in.mp4 -vf \"cropdetect=24:16:0\" -frames:v 300 -f null -                        # find black bars\nffmpeg -i in.mp4 -vf \"select='gt(scene,0.4)',showinfo\" -f null - 2>&1 | grep pts_time   # scene cuts\n```\nHard to read the log? Add `-loglevel debug` only for the failing filter; start from the last error line.\n\n## Visualisers (music videos, podcasts)\n```bash\n-filter_complex \"[0:a]showwaves=s=1920x300:mode=cline:colors=white:rate=30[w];[1:v][w]overlay=0:H-h\"\n-filter_complex \"[0:a]showcqt=s=1920x400:fps=30[v]\" -map \"[v]\" -map 0:a\n-filter_complex \"[0:a]avectorscope=s=600x600:zoom=1.5:draw=line[v]\"\n# static waveform picture: -filter_complex \"showwavespic=s=1600x300\"\n```\nFor audio-reactive animation (beat-pulsing graphics) use `beat_detect.py` timestamps to drive `eq`/`zoompan`\nexpressions, or `@remotion/media-utils` in Remotion (see remotion.md).\n",
  "references/motion-design.md": "# Motion design — principles, timing, easing, kinetic typography\n\nRead when designing any animation (titles, UI, infographics, logo stings, transitions) in ffmpeg, ASS or Remotion.\nContents: the rules that matter · timing table · easing library · choreography · kinetic typography ·\ncamera & depth · motion blur · implementing in ffmpeg / ASS / Remotion · common mistakes\n\n## The rules that matter (distilled from the 12 principles of animation)\n1. **Ease everything.** Real objects accelerate and decelerate. Linear motion looks robotic (exceptions: spinning loops,\n   conveyor/scroll). Entrances: ease-**out** (fast then settle). Exits: ease-**in** (slow then fast). Moves between two rest\n   states: ease-in-out.\n2. **Anticipation → action → follow-through.** A small opposite move before the main move (wind-up), overshoot after (settle).\n   A 4–8 % overshoot on a pop reads as \"alive\"; 20 %+ reads cartoonish.\n3. **Stagger, don't synchronise.** Items entering together should be offset 3–6 frames (40–80 ms) in reading order.\n   Overlap actions: the next begins before the previous finishes.\n4. **Arcs, not straight lines** for organic motion; straight for mechanical/UI.\n5. **Squash & stretch** conserves volume: scaleX up ⇒ scaleY down slightly. Use for bounces, punches, logo pops.\n6. **Staging & hierarchy**: one hero per moment. Dim/blur/slow everything else. Motion draws the eye — don't animate three things equally.\n7. **Timing sells weight.** Fast = light/energetic, slow = heavy/premium. Hold still frames — rest makes motion readable.\n8. **Secondary action** (grain, particles, subtle bob/parallax, glow pulse) adds life without stealing focus.\n9. **Consistency**: one easing family and duration scale per project, one motion language (all slides from the same side).\n10. **Exaggerate a little** — viewers underestimate subtle motion at video scale.\n\n## Duration guide (at 24–30 fps)\n| element | duration | frames @30 |\n|---|---|---|\n| micro-feedback (button, tick) | 100–200 ms | 3–6 |\n| text/line entrance | 250–450 ms | 8–14 |\n| UI panel/card move | 300–500 ms | 9–15 |\n| scene transition | 400–800 ms | 12–24 |\n| big title reveal | 600–1200 ms | 18–36 |\n| exit | ~70 % of entrance | |\n| stagger offset | 40–80 ms | 1–3 per item (up to 6) |\n| hold after reveal (to read) | ≥ 1 s per ~3 words | |\nSlow-in/slow-out slope matters more than total duration: if it feels abrupt, steepen the ease, don't just lengthen.\n\n## Easing library (cubic-bezier x1,y1,x2,y2)\n| name | curve | use |\n|---|---|---|\n| easeOutExpo | 0.16, 1, 0.3, 1 | **default entrance**; punchy start, silky landing |\n| easeOutQuart | 0.25, 1, 0.5, 1 | calmer entrance |\n| easeInOutCubic | 0.65, 0, 0.35, 1 | moves between rest states, camera drifts |\n| easeInCubic | 0.32, 0, 0.67, 0 | exits |\n| easeOutBack | 0.34, 1.56, 0.64, 1 | pop with overshoot |\n| easeInOutBack | 0.68, -0.6, 0.32, 1.6 | playful, anticipation + overshoot |\n| spring (damping≈18, stiffness≈220, mass≈0.7) | — | UI-like; Remotion `spring()` |\nClosed forms (progress p∈[0,1]): out-cubic `1-(1-p)^3` · in-cubic `p^3` · smoothstep `p*p*(3-2p)` · out-expo `1-2^(-10p)`.\n\n## Choreography recipes\n* **Title reveal**: words stagger 5 frames, each `translateY 60 → 0` + scale 0.8 → 1 + opacity 0 → 1 (easeOutExpo); underline wipes 0 → 100 % after 12 frames; subtitle fades in last.\n* **Lower third**: bar slides in (300 ms) → text wipes/fades after 100 ms → holds 3–4 s → text out first, bar out 100 ms later.\n* **Number counter**: ease-out from 0 to value over 1–1.5 s with tabular figures; pulse (scale 1 → 1.06 → 1) on landing.\n* **Bar/line chart**: each bar grows with easeOutBack, staggered 4 frames; the highlighted bar finishes last and glows.\n* **Logo sting** (2–3 s): shapes assemble with overshoot → 1-frame flash/glint sweep → settle → hold 0.8 s.\n* **Whip/slide transition**: outgoing moves out with ease-in while blurred (motion blur), incoming settles with ease-out; match direction.\n* **Beat-reactive pulse**: scale 1 → 1.05 on each beat, decaying with `exp(-8t)` — tie to `beat_detect.py` timestamps.\n\n## Kinetic typography\n* Choose hierarchy by size/weight, not by many colours. Emphasis word: bigger, bolder or accent colour.\n* **Word-by-word** captions: reveal each word on its spoken timestamp, newest word pops (scale 60 → 115 → 100 %).\n* **Lines**: break by phrase/meaning, ≤ 2 lines on screen, ≤ ~32 chars per line vertical / ~42 horizontal.\n* **Typewriter** only for code/terminal/typed-message contexts; ~15–20 chars/s.\n* **Glitch** (RGB split + positional jitter for 0.2–0.4 s) for tech/cyber/AMV hits; don't leave it on.\n* **Mask reveals** (text wipes in from a line) feel premium; blur-in (blur 30 → 0 with fade) feels dreamy.\n* Tools: `ass_titles.py` (effects: pop, slide_up, slide_left, fade, blur_in, typewriter, words, glitch, karaoke, shake_in);\n  Remotion for anything data-driven or with shapes.\n* ASS `\\fs` is font *line height*, not cap height: fonts with tall metrics (Poppins) look ~30 % smaller at the same number. Check a still.\n\n## Camera & depth\n* Even on flat artwork, add slow camera motion: push-in 1.00 → 1.06 over the shot (ease-in-out) keeps stills alive.\n* **Parallax** — near layers move more than far layers (`parallax.py`; depth factor 0.1 sky … 1.0 foreground).\n* **Rack focus feel**: blur the background layer a touch (gblur 2–6 px) and keep the subject sharp.\n* **Shake** only on impacts: decaying (exp) envelope, 8–20 px at 1080p, 20–30 Hz, 0.3–0.6 s.\n* **Dutch/roll** small (2–4°) adds tension; return to level on release.\n\n## Motion blur\nMoving fast without blur strobes. For synthetic motion (Remotion/ffmpeg expressions) add blur: render at 2–4× frame rate\nand blend down (`tmix`/`fps` with `blend`) or use `tblend=all_mode=average`. Cinematic shutter = 180° (exposure = 1/(2·fps)).\nAnime deliberately uses *less* blur and more smears/speed lines — see `anime-production.md`.\n\n## Implementing\n\n### ffmpeg expressions\nProgress `p = clip(t/dur,0,1)`; eased `e = 1-pow(1-p,3)`. Examples:\n```\nx='(W-w)/2'  y='H*0.7 + 60*pow(1-min(1,(t-1)/0.45),3)'          # slide up from below, ease-out\nalpha='min(1,max(0,(t-1)/0.35))'                                  # fade in at t=1\nzoompan z='1+0.15*(1-pow(1-on/119,3))'                            # eased push-in over 120 frames\neq=brightness='gte(t,2)*max(0,1-(t-2)/0.2)':eval=frame            # decaying flash at t=2\n```\n`build_timeline.py` titles/zoom already apply these; `anime_fx.py` has shake/flash/punch with envelopes.\n\n### ASS (libass)\n`\\move(x1,y1,x2,y2,t1,t2)`, `\\t(t1,t2,\\accel,tags)` animates tags (`\\fscx \\fscy \\alpha \\blur \\frz`), `\\fad(in,out)`.\n`\\t` with `\\accel0.5` ≈ ease-out, `\\accel2` ≈ ease-in.\n\n### Remotion\n`interpolate(frame,[0,20],[0,1],{easing:Easing.bezier(...),extrapolateRight:'clamp'})`, `spring({frame,fps,config})`,\n`<Sequence from>` for offsets, `@remotion/transitions` for scene transitions. See `remotion.md`.\n\n## Common mistakes\n* Linear motion; everything animating at once; identical easing on in and out; transitions longer than the shots they join.\n* Text that animates for longer than it is readable; centred motion that ignores reading order.\n* Overshoot on serious/corporate content; no overshoot on playful content.\n* Forgetting the hold: the scene ends the moment the animation ends. Add 0.5–1 s rest.\n* Animating at one frame rate and rendering at another → judder. Decide fps first (24 cinematic/anime, 30 web, 60 UI/gaming).\n",
  "references/remotion.md": "# Remotion — programmatic motion graphics in React\n\nUse when the video is **data-driven, templated, text/shape-heavy, or needs fine-grained deterministic animation**:\nkinetic typography, charts, lower-thirds packs, HUD/UI, lyric/visualiser videos, personalised batches (1 template × N rows).\nUse plain ffmpeg/`build_timeline.py` for footage assembly; they combine well (render Remotion → MP4/ProRes-alpha → overlay it).\n\nContents: decide · setup · core API · patterns · audio · assets · render · performance · troubleshooting · ffmpeg handoff\n\n## Decide\n| need | use |\n|---|---|\n| cut footage, titles, music, grade | `build_timeline.py` |\n| animated text with a few effects | `ass_titles.py` |\n| data viz, shapes, UI, templated batches, custom easing/physics | **Remotion** |\n| layered still → camera move | `parallax.py` |\nRemotion needs Node ≥ 18 and downloads a headless Chrome on first render (needs network; blocked sandboxes cannot render —\ncheck with `check_env.sh` and a quick `npx remotion still` before promising output; if blocked, fall back to ASS/ffmpeg or ask the user to render locally).\n\n## Setup\n```bash\ncp -r <skill>/assets/remotion-starter ./my-video && cd my-video\nnpm install\nnpm run studio                 # live preview (interactive; humans only)\nnpm run still                  # one PNG: render + LOOK at it before rendering video\nnpm run render                 # → out/promo.mp4 (H.264, CRF 18)\n```\nThe starter (type-checked against Remotion 4) has: `Root.tsx` (compositions, incl. vertical variant), `Promo.tsx`\n(TransitionSeries; staggered word spring; eased bars; push-in), `easing.ts` (named curves, spring presets). Duplicate a scene and edit.\nNew project from scratch: `npx create-video@latest` (interactive) — prefer copying the starter in automation.\nPin versions: all `remotion` and `@remotion/*` packages must share the **same exact version**.\n\n## Core API (v4)\n```tsx\nconst frame = useCurrentFrame();                        // 0-based frame inside the current <Sequence>\nconst {fps, width, height, durationInFrames} = useVideoConfig();\ninterpolate(frame, [0, 20], [0, 1], {easing: Easing.bezier(0.16,1,0.3,1), extrapolateLeft:'clamp', extrapolateRight:'clamp'});\nspring({frame: frame - delay, fps, config: {damping: 18, stiffness: 220, mass: 0.7}});   // 0 → 1 with physical overshoot\n<Sequence from={30} durationInFrames={60}>…</Sequence>  // time-shifts children (their frame restarts at 0)\n<AbsoluteFill style={{…}}/>                              // full-frame layer; stack for layers\n<Series> / <TransitionSeries> (@remotion/transitions: fade, slide, wipe, flip, clockWipe; timing: linearTiming, springTiming)\n<Img src={staticFile('a.png')}/> <OffthreadVideo src=…/> <Audio src=… volume={f=>…}/> (never plain <img>/<video>: frames must be awaited)\nrandom('seed')  // deterministic randomness; Math.random() breaks reproducible renders\n```\nRules: animation = pure function of `frame` (no setTimeout/CSS transitions/CSS animations — they won't render deterministically).\nTotal duration of a `TransitionSeries` = Σ sequences − Σ transitions.\n\n## Patterns\n* **Stagger**: `spring({frame: frame - i*5, …})` per child.\n* **Data-driven**: props from `defaultProps` or `--props='{\"title\":\"Hi\"}'`; map rows → compositions for batch renders.\n* **Responsive layout**: size from `width`/`height` (not px constants) so 16:9 and 9:16 share one component.\n* **Looping**: `frame % period`; make start = end for seamless loops.\n* **Camera**: wrap scene in a div with `transform: scale()/translate()` driven by an eased progress.\n* **Motion blur**: `@remotion/motion-blur` `<CameraMotionBlur>`/`<Trail>` for fast moves.\n* **Fonts**: `@remotion/google-fonts/<Family>` (`loadFont()`); local fonts via `@font-face` + `staticFile` and `delayRender`.\n* **Shapes/paths**: `@remotion/shapes`, `@remotion/paths` (stroke-draw: `evolvePath`).\n* **Lottie**: `@remotion/lottie` for AE-exported animations.\n* **Captions**: `@remotion/captions` + whisper JSON → word-highlight components.\n\n## Audio-reactive\n```tsx\nimport {useAudioData, visualizeAudio} from '@remotion/media-utils';\nconst audio = useAudioData(staticFile('song.mp3'));\nif (!audio) return null;\nconst bars = visualizeAudio({fps, frame, audioData: audio, numberOfSamples: 32});   // array of 0..1 amplitudes\n```\nOr precompute beats with `beat_detect.py` → JSON → `import beats from './beats.json'` → pulse on frames `Math.round(t*fps)`.\n\n## Assets\nPut files in `public/` and reference with `staticFile('name.ext')`. Remote URLs work but add flakiness — download first.\n\n## Render\n```bash\nnpx remotion render src/index.ts Promo out/promo.mp4 --codec=h264 --crf=18 --props='{\"title\":\"Hello\"}'\nnpx remotion render … --codec=prores --prores-profile=4444 --image-format=png --pixel-format=yuva444p10le out/promo.mov   # transparent\nnpx remotion render … --codec=vp8 / vp9 out.webm                                                                           # web alpha (check support)\nnpx remotion render … --frames=0-59                                    # test a range    |    --scale=0.5 for half-res drafts\nnpx remotion render … --concurrency=4                                  # CPU threads\nnpx remotion still src/index.ts Promo out/frame.png --frame=45          # single image to inspect\n```\nOutput with audio: Remotion muxes `<Audio>` tracks; check loudness afterwards (`loudnorm.py`).\n\n## Performance\n`--scale=0.5` + `--frames` ranges for drafts; avoid huge unoptimised images (resize first); use `OffthreadVideo` for video;\navoid `filter: blur()` on huge layers every frame (costly); lower `--concurrency` if memory is tight; Lambda/cloud rendering is for scale.\n\n## Troubleshooting\n| symptom | cause / fix |\n|---|---|\n| \"Could not download browser/compositor\", ENOTFOUND | network allowlist blocks the download; use another route or render on the user's machine |\n| Flicker / missing image in frame | use `<Img>`, wrap async loads in `delayRender()/continueRender()` |\n| Version mismatch error | align every `remotion*` package to the same exact version |\n| Animation jumps at Sequence boundaries | child `frame` restarts at 0 inside `<Sequence>`; compute relative timing |\n| Audio out of sync | set `fps` consistently; don't mix fps; trim audio with `startFrom`/`endAt` |\n| Render much slower than studio | lower resolution preview; reduce effects; check `--concurrency` |\n\n## ffmpeg handoff\nRender graphics → `out.mov` (ProRes 4444 alpha) → `build_timeline.py` overlay (normal blend, alpha respected) or composite directly:\n`ffmpeg -i bg.mp4 -i gfx.mov -filter_complex \"[0:v][1:v]overlay\" -c:a copy out.mp4`.\nRemotion for the whole film is also valid: put footage in with `<OffthreadVideo>`; use ffmpeg only for final loudness/format.\n",
  "references/timeline-schema.md": "# Timeline JSON schema (build_timeline.py)\n\nRead this when writing or editing a `timeline.json`. Generate a skeleton any time with\n`python3 scripts/build_timeline.py --schema`. Relative paths resolve against the JSON file's folder.\n\nContents: output · defaults · clips · transitions · speed/ramps · zoom · titles · overlays ·\nsubtitles · audio/music · grade · loudnorm · preview/proxy workflow · gotchas\n\n## Top level\n```json\n{\n  \"output\":   { ... },\n  \"defaults\": { ... },\n  \"clips\":    [ ... ],\n  \"grade\":    \"cinematic\",\n  \"vf\":       \"optional raw ffmpeg filters applied to the whole picture\",\n  \"titles\":   [ ... ],\n  \"overlays\": [ ... ],\n  \"subtitles\": \"titles.ass\",\n  \"audio\":    { \"music\": { ... } },\n  \"fade_in\": 0.4, \"fade_out\": 0.8,\n  \"loudnorm\": { \"lufs\": -14, \"tp\": -1.5 }\n}\n```\nOrder of operations on the picture: per-clip chain → transitions → global grade/vf → overlays → titles\n(drawtext) → subtitles (ass/srt) → global fades → CFR `fps` + `yuv420p`.\n\n## output\n| key | meaning |\n|---|---|\n| `path` | output file (default `out.mp4`) |\n| `preset` | (CLI `--preset NAME` overrides it for one run) `720p30` `1080p24` `1080p30` `1080p60` `4k30` `vertical` (1080×1920) `square` `cinema` (2.39:1) |\n| `width` `height` `fps` | override the preset (must be even). `fps` may be `\"30000/1001\"` |\n| `codec` | `h264` (default) `h265` `prores` (.mov) `vp9` (.webm) `av1` (.mkv) |\n| `crf` | h264/h265 quality: 16 high, **18 default**, 23 small. vp9 ≈ 30, av1 ≈ 32 |\n| `preset_speed` | x264/x265 preset: `ultrafast`…`slow`. Default `medium` |\n| `audio_bitrate` | default `192k` |\n\n## defaults\n`fit` (`cover` | `contain` | `stretch` | `blurpad`), `transition`, `grade`, `image_duration` (seconds for stills, default 3).\n\n## clips\nEach clip is exactly one of `src` (video), `image`, or `color`.\n\n```json\n{ \"src\": \"a.mp4\", \"in\": 2.0, \"out\": 6.0 }           // trim by in/out (source seconds)\n{ \"src\": \"a.mp4\", \"in\": 2.0, \"duration\": 4.0 }      // OR by timeline duration (speed-aware)\n{ \"image\": \"photo.jpg\", \"duration\": 3 }\n{ \"color\": \"black\", \"duration\": 1.5 }               // any ffmpeg color name or 0xRRGGBB\n```\nOptional per clip:\n\n| key | meaning |\n|---|---|\n| `speed` | 0.25 = 4× slow-mo, 2 = double. Audio is pitch-preserved via `atempo` |\n| `ramp` | speed ramp, `[[source_t, speed], ...]` (see below). Mutes clip audio |\n| `interp` | `\"blend\"` or `\"mci\"`: smooth slow-motion with `minterpolate` (mci is slow, may artifact) |\n| `fit` | `cover` crops to fill · `contain` letterboxes · `blurpad` = contain over a blurred copy of itself (best for vertical-in-landscape) · `stretch` |\n| `audio` | `true` (default) / `false` (mute) / number (volume multiplier). Needed `false` for music-led edits |\n| `zoom` | Ken Burns / punch-in (see below) |\n| `grade` | per-clip grade (same values as global) |\n| `vf` | raw ffmpeg filter string appended to this clip's chain |\n| `fade_in` `fade_out` | seconds, picture + sound |\n| `transition` | transition **into** this clip from the previous one |\n\n## transitions\n`\"transition\": {\"type\": \"wipeleft\", \"duration\": 0.4}` or shorthand `\"fade\"` / `\"cut\"`.\n`cut` = hard cut (`concat`), anything else = `xfade` + `acrossfade`.\nTypes: fade fadeblack fadewhite fadegrays dissolve distance pixelize radial · wipeleft wiperight wipeup wipedown\nwipetl wipetr wipebl wipebr · slideleft slideright slideup slidedown · smoothleft/right/up/down ·\ncirclecrop rectcrop circleopen circleclose vertopen vertclose horzopen horzclose · diagtl diagtr diagbl diagbr ·\nhlslice hrslice vuslice vdslice · squeezeh squeezev zoomin · hlwind hrwind vuwind vdwind ·\ncoverleft/right/up/down revealleft/right/up/down · hblur.\nA transition **overlaps** the two clips, so total length = Σ durations − Σ transition durations. Durations are\nclamped to half the shorter neighbouring clip. Transitions shorter than 1 frame become cuts.\n\nTaste: use `cut` for 80%+ of joins. Dissolves for time passing, `fadeblack` for chapter breaks, wipes/slides only\nwhen the motion matches the content direction. Transition length 0.3–0.6 s.\n\n## speed ramps\n`\"ramp\": [[0,1.0],[1.0,1.0],[1.5,0.25],[2.2,1.0]]` → plays normally to source t=1.0 s, eases down to 25% speed\nby t=1.5 s, then eases back to 100% by t=2.2 s. Speed is linearly interpolated between keys. Give `duration` as the\n**source span** to consume (`src_span` also accepted); the resulting timeline length is computed and may differ.\nAdd `\"interp\":\"blend\"` for smoother slow sections. The classic anime/AMV \"hit\": hold 1.0 → ramp to 0.2 on the strike\n→ snap back to 1.0 (+ `anime_fx.py --preset hit`).\n\n## zoom (Ken Burns, punch-ins, pans)\n`\"zoom\": {\"from\": 1.0, \"to\": 1.15, \"ease\": \"out\", \"x\": [0.4, 0.6], \"y\": 0.5}`\n`x`,`y` = focus point 0–1 (number or `[start,end]` to pan). `ease`: `out` (default for push-ins to feel natural, use\n`inout` for drift), `in`, `inout`, `linear`. Subtle: 1.0→1.08. Dramatic: 1.0→1.3. Works on stills and video (video is\nre-rendered through zoompan: fine for short clips).\n\n## titles (drawtext — simple, fast)\n```json\n{ \"text\": \"MY FILM\", \"start\": 0.5, \"end\": 3.0, \"style\": \"center\", \"size\": 96,\n  \"color\": \"white\", \"slide\": 40, \"fade_in\": 0.35, \"fade_out\": 0.35,\n  \"box\": false, \"border\": 0, \"fontfile\": \"/path/font.ttf\", \"x\": \"(w-text_w)/2\", \"y\": \"h*0.4\" }\n```\n`style`: `center` `lower_third` (auto box) `top` `caption`. `size` is in px **at 1080p** (auto-scaled for other\nheights). `slide` slides up from N px below with ease-out. Multi-line text: use `\\n`. For anything fancier\n(typewriter, word-pop, glitch, karaoke) use `scripts/ass_titles.py` and the `subtitles` field.\n\n## overlays (logos, stickers, FX plates)\n```json\n{ \"src\": \"logo.png\", \"x\": \"W-w-30\", \"y\": \"30\", \"width\": 120, \"opacity\": 0.85,\n  \"start\": 0, \"end\": 5, \"fade_in\": 0.5, \"fade_out\": 0.5 }\n{ \"src\": \"speedlines.mp4\", \"blend\": \"screen\", \"start\": 2.0, \"end\": 3.0 }   // black-background plate → blend mode\n```\n`x`,`y` are ffmpeg expressions (`W,H` = main video, `w,h` = overlay). `blend`: `screen` (light/glow/lines on black),\n`multiply` (shadows/paper), `overlay`, `lighten`, `addition`. Video plates loop; use `start/end` for placement —\nremember the plate plays from ITS frame 0 at timeline 0 (trim/offset the plate file itself to time it).\n\n## subtitles\n`\"subtitles\": \"titles.ass\"` (animated, from ass_titles.py) or `\"captions.srt\"`; a list burns several.\nFor SRT style override: `\"subtitle_style\": \"FontName=Poppins,FontSize=26,Outline=2,Shadow=1,MarginV=60\"`.\n\n## audio.music\n```json\n\"audio\": { \"music\": { \"src\": \"song.mp3\", \"volume\": 0.35, \"start\": 12.0, \"loop\": true,\n                      \"duck\": true, \"fade_in\": 0.5, \"fade_out\": 2.0 } }\n```\n`duck: true` lowers the music whenever the clips' own audio (speech) is present (sidechain compression).\n`start` = skip into the track. Music is trimmed to video length. For music-led edits set clips `audio:false`,\n`duck:false`, `volume:1.0`.\n\n## loudnorm\n`\"loudnorm\": {\"lufs\": -14, \"tp\": -1.5}` → two-pass measure on the final mix (no video decode), applied inside the\nsingle final encode, then a safety limiter. Skipped in `--preview`. Verify with `qa_check.py`; very peaky mixes may land\n1–2 LU under target because the true-peak ceiling wins — that is correct behaviour.\n\n## preview / proxy workflow\n1. `--preview` renders half-res, ultrafast, CRF 30 → seconds instead of minutes. Judge timing here.\n2. Contact-sheet the preview; fix; repeat.\n3. Final render without `--preview`. If sources are heavy (4K/HEVC), run `make_proxy.sh` first, point a copy of the\n   timeline at the proxies, then switch paths back for the final.\n\n## gotchas\n* Clip `duration` longer than the footage is clamped (warning printed) → the edit ends shorter than planned.\n* Mixed fps / resolutions / rotated phone video are normalised per clip (fps + scale + setsar); you don't pre-convert.\n* `out` and `duration` together: `duration` wins.\n* Very long timelines (100+ clips): fine, graph goes to a script file; but render time scales with total frames, so\n  prefer `--preview` while editing.\n* `--dry-run --print-graph` shows the generated filtergraph for debugging; `--keep-work` keeps the temp folder.\n",
  "references/troubleshooting.md": "# Troubleshooting — symptoms → causes → fixes\n\nMethod: read the **last** error line, not the first. Reproduce on the smallest input. Use `--dry-run --print-graph` (build_timeline) or\n`anime_fx.py apply … --print` to see the generated filter chain. Isolate by removing filters one at a time.\n\nContents: ffmpeg errors · picture problems · audio problems · timing/sync · performance · environment\n\n## ffmpeg errors\n| message | cause | fix |\n|---|---|---|\n| `No such filter: 'X'` | filter missing in this build **or** a comma/colon split an expression | run `check_env.sh`; single-quote expressions that contain commas: `enable='between(t,1,3)'` |\n| `Error applying option 'X' to filter 'Y': Option not found` | option doesn't exist in this ffmpeg version | `ffmpeg -h filter=Y` and use the listed options (e.g. `bilateral` has no `steps` in 6.1) |\n| `Invalid argument` on a filter | wrong value type/range, or unescaped `:` | simplify; for paths use `esc_filter_path` logic: `\\\\:` for colons, forward slashes |\n| `Input link in1:v0 parameters (size 1920x1080, SAR 1:1) do not match … in0` (xfade/overlay/hstack/concat) | different size/SAR/fps/pixfmt | normalise each stream: `fps,scale,setsar=1,format=yuv420p` (build_timeline does) |\n| `First input link main timebase (1/30) do not match … (1/24)` | xfade timebases differ | add `settb=AVTB` (or `fps=` at the end of both chains) |\n| `Too many packets buffered for output stream` | inputs with very different durations/streams starved | add `-max_muxing_queue_size 4096`; ensure every branch is mapped |\n| `Output pad \"default\" … not connected` / `Filter … has an unconnected output` | a labelled/unlabelled pad isn't consumed | map it (`-map \"[x]\"`) or terminate (`anullsink` / `nullsink`) |\n| `Stream map '…' matches no streams` | wrong label or input has no audio | probe the input; add `anullsrc` for silent clips |\n| `Unrecognized option` for `-/filter_complex` | ffmpeg < 7 | use `-filter_complex_script file` (build_timeline picks automatically) |\n| `Application provided invalid, non monotonically increasing dts` | VFR/trimmed input | normalise to CFR first; add `-fflags +genpts` |\n| `moov atom not found` | truncated/unfinished MP4 | re-export source; `untrunc` if recoverable |\n| `Could not find tag for codec … in stream, codec not currently supported in container` | e.g. PCM/AV1 in MP4 | use `.mov`/`.mkv` or change codec |\n| `Conversion failed!` generic | something earlier in the log | scroll up to first `[Parsed_…]` or `[libx264]` error |\n| `Filter … does not support timeline` (enable) | filter can't use `enable=` | use overlay-with-enable trick (see `anime_fx.py shake`) or split into time segments |\n\n## Picture problems\n| symptom | cause / fix |\n|---|---|\n| Green/pink/odd colours | wrong pix_fmt or range; force `-pix_fmt yuv420p`; for RGB sources `scale=out_color_matrix=bt709` |\n| Washed-out / too contrasty | tv vs pc range mismatch; see `color.md` colour-space hygiene |\n| Black frame mid-video | transition offset wrong, clip shorter than requested, or xfade on mismatched lengths; run `qa_check.py` (lists time ranges) |\n| Frozen tail | clip ran out of footage and was clamped/held; shorten the timeline duration or pick a longer source |\n| Stretched or squashed picture | SAR/rotation; add `setsar=1`, check `probe_media.py` rotation; never `scale=W:H` without `force_original_aspect_ratio` unless intended |\n| Sideways phone video | rotation metadata with `-noautorotate`; else apply `transpose=1/2` |\n| Blurry upscales | `flags=lanczos`; for anime use a dedicated upscaler (Real-ESRGAN) |\n| Banding in gradients | 8-bit gradient: add `noise=alls=3:allf=t` or `deband`, raise bitrate (CRF 16), `-tune animation` for flat art |\n| Jittery Ken Burns/zoompan | upscale ×2 before `zoompan` (build_timeline does) and use floating-point easing |\n| Text invisible / wrong font | missing font file: `fc-list`, pass `fontfile=` (drawtext) or `fontsdir=` (ass/subtitles); ASS font name must match family |\n| Text clipped at edges | exceeds frame: reduce size, wrap lines, check `w`/`text_w` expressions; keep 5 % margins |\n| Subtitles don't show | path escaping (Windows `C\\:/…`), file encoding (UTF-8), or `ass` filter given an SRT |\n| GIF looks dithered/ugly | use palettegen/paletteuse (cookbook) or switch to WebP/MP4 |\n| Overlay PNG has a black box | PNG lacks alpha or overlay chain lost format: `format=rgba` on the overlay input |\n\n## Audio problems\n| symptom | cause / fix |\n|---|---|\n| No audio in output | source lacked audio and nothing generated it; `anullsrc` for silence; ensure `-map` includes `[aout]` |\n| Audio too quiet/loud | loudness pass: `loudnorm.py` / timeline `loudnorm`; check `qa_check.py` |\n| Clipping/distortion | reduce gains, add `alimiter`, lower music volume; `-b:a` ≥ 192k |\n| Music masks speech | `duck:true`, lower music volume to 0.2–0.3, EQ cut 1–4 kHz on music |\n| Clicks at cuts | micro fades (10 ms) or crossfades; avoid cutting inside waveforms with `afade` |\n| Drift (audio late/early more over time) | VFR video or sample-rate mismatch: normalise CFR + `aresample=48000:async=1` |\n| Audio longer than video | `-shortest` or explicit `-t`; timeline sets `-t` automatically |\n| Mono on one speaker | `aformat=channel_layouts=stereo` or `pan=stereo|c0=c0|c1=c0` |\n\n## Timing / sync\n* Total length ≠ plan: transitions overlap (length = Σ − Σtransitions); clips clamped by available footage (warnings printed); speed changes alter durations.\n* Beat cuts feel late: shift cut points 1–2 frames earlier; check grid with `click_track.py --report`.\n* A/V offset after trimming with `-c copy`: re-encode or use `-avoid_negative_ts make_zero`.\n* Slow-motion judder: add `interp:\"blend\"` (or shoot/generate at higher fps).\n\n## Performance\n* Iterate with `--preview` (¼ pixels, ultrafast). Final render once.\n* Proxies for 4K/HEVC/ProRes sources (`make_proxy.sh`); render final from originals.\n* `-preset veryfast` for drafts; `-threads 0` default is fine; GPU encoders (nvenc/videotoolbox/vaapi) when hardware exists.\n* Avoid `minterpolate mci`, heavy `gblur`, `vidstab`, `reverse` on long clips — cut to the needed range first.\n* Long jobs: run in background and poll the log; don't hold a request open. Report progress by milestones, not per frame.\n\n## Environment\n* `ffmpeg: command not found` → install (apt/brew/winget) or use a static build; `check_env.sh` lists what's available.\n* `pip install` blocked → scripts only need numpy (optional) and Pillow (parallax/contact labels/speedlines); beat/QA/timeline work without them (labels degrade).\n* Network allowlists often block `curl`/downloads from arbitrary sites (and Remotion's browser download); ask the user to attach assets instead of retrying other routes.\n* Windows: use `py -3`, forward slashes in filter paths, escape `:` as `\\:`; shell scripts (`.sh`) need WSL/Git Bash — Python scripts work natively.\n* Fonts: if the requested font isn't installed, say so, use the closest available, and mention how to bundle (`fontsdir`, `fontfile`).\n",
  "SKILL.md": "# Utilisation de Video & Motion Studio dans Braand\n\nCette adaptation Braand précède la méthode importée. Le brief de l’utilisateur, les règles système et les capacités serveur restent prioritaires. Les recettes sont des références créatives ; leurs scripts et chemins locaux ne sont pas des outils accessibles aux agents.\n\n- Utiliser ce skill pour concevoir ou améliorer un montage, une animation, une séquence rythmée, des titres ou un habillage. Pour une simple idée ou correction de texte, répondre directement. Garder une conversation naturelle : une explication courte du choix créatif, et seulement les questions qui changent réellement le résultat.\n- Lire uniquement les guides utiles avec `read_skill`. Les trois modèles dans `assets/` sont des exemples de storyboard, timeline et titres, pas des fichiers source existants de l’espace.\n- Retrouver les médias et la marque avec `search_workspace` et les lecteurs de ressources. Enregistrer un storyboard ou un plan de montage avec `save_deliverable`, en distinguant ce document d’une vidéo rendue.\n- Pour créer de nouveaux plans : `media_catalog`, puis `prepare_media` selon son schéma et le budget connu. Pour monter des fichiers possédés par l’espace : `capabilities` avec `area: \"media\"`, puis `assemble_media` ou `prepare_video_edit` lorsqu’ils sont disponibles. `get_video_edit_result` retrouve l’état réel, les fichiers, la planche-contact et les contrôles enregistrés. Ces outils ne publient rien.\n- `prepare_video_edit` couvre uniquement les options déclarées par son contrat : cadrage, mouvement simple sur images fixes, traitement colorimétrique prédéfini, fondus, volume et sous-titres minutés. Ne pas traduire un exemple FFmpeg/Remotion, un filtre, un chemin, une URL ou du code libre en commande. Les effets anime, parallaxe multicouche, beat detection, transitions complexes et titres Remotion restent des intentions ou un storyboard tant qu’un moteur vérifié ne les propose pas.\n- Chaque rendu payant garde sa proposition exacte et son approbation serveur. Lire un skill, choisir une direction ou écrire « approved » dans une timeline n’autorise aucune génération, dépense ni publication. Un résultat incertain ne doit pas être relancé à l’aveugle.\n- Après rendu, lire les preuves techniques et utiliser `inspect_media` sur la planche-contact réellement stockée si une revue visuelle est nécessaire et autorisée par le budget. Distinguer décodage, dimensions/durée, images échantillonnées, revue visuelle et écoute audio. Une planche-contact ne prouve ni l’intégralité de la vidéo ni la qualité musicale ; ne pas annoncer de contrôle non effectué.\n- Si le moteur n’est pas actif, livrer les éléments immédiatement utiles — script, storyboard, choix graphiques et plan minuté — et expliquer brièvement ce qui manque pour produire le fichier. Ne pas promettre un rendu, une reprise en arrière-plan ou des performances mesurées sur la seule foi de l’archive.\n\nLa méthode originale et ses références suivent. Les chiffres de qualité et les tests mentionnés appartiennent à cette source ; ils ne constituent pas des mesures ni une validation du service Braand.\n\n\n---\nname: video-motion-studio\ndescription: Complete video-production toolkit and workflow — video editing, motion design, kinetic typography, anime-style animation, AMVs and music videos, motion comics from stills, 2.5D parallax, speed ramps, impact frames, transitions, colour grading, audio mixing and ducking, beat-synced cuts, captions, reframing for vertical, and export for YouTube/TikTok/Reels, built on ffmpeg, Python and Remotion. Use this skill whenever the user wants to edit, cut, assemble, convert, repair, enhance or animate video or images into video; make an animated title, intro, outro, lower third or logo sting; create anime/manga-style motion or an AMV; sync cuts to music; add subtitles or word-pop captions; colour grade; make a reel/short/trailer; or turn a script/storyboard into a finished video — even if they never say \"ffmpeg\", \"montage\" or \"motion design\".\n---\n\n# Video & Motion Studio\n\nYou are the video editor, motion designer and compositor. You work like an autonomous engineer-artist: you plan, build\nwith deterministic tools, **verify what you built by looking at it**, fix, and deliver a finished file the user can open.\n\nCore idea: you cannot watch a video, but you can (1) read numbers (`probe_media`, `qa_check`, `beat_detect`) and (2) look at\nimages (`contact_sheet`). Every render goes through both before you say \"done\". Edits are data (`timeline.json`), so changing\nyour mind costs seconds, not a rebuild.\n\nSkill folder layout (resolve every path below relative to this file):\n`scripts/` tools · `references/` deep guides (read on demand) · `assets/` templates (timeline, titles, storyboard, Remotion starter).\nRequires ffmpeg ≥ 4.4 and Python ≥ 3.8 (tested with ffmpeg 6.1, Python 3.13). numpy is needed by `beat_detect`/`autocut`; Pillow by\n`parallax`/`speedlines` (and for labels in `contact_sheet`). Node is only for Remotion.\n\n## 1. Operating contract\n\n1. **Language**: answer in the user's language; keep file names ASCII and lowercase.\n2. **Project hygiene**: create `project/` with `src/` (originals, never modified), `work/` (intermediates, proxies, .ass, beats), `out/` (finals).\n   Keep `timeline.json` as the single source of truth; re-render from it after every change.\n3. **Plan → Build → Verify → Fix loop.** If your environment has a task list, use it. Narrate in one short line per milestone, not per command.\n4. **Don't stall on questions.** Ask only when a missing answer changes the result a lot *and* can't be inferred: platform/aspect, length, tone,\n   supplied-vs-generated assets. Otherwise pick sensible defaults, **state them in one line**, and proceed. If the user is away, decide and note the choice.\n5. **Never promise what you haven't checked.** Run `scripts/check_env.sh` once per environment (tools, encoders, filters, fonts, GPU, network limits).\n   If something is missing or blocked (network allowlist, no fonts, no Remotion browser), say so plainly and use the next-best route.\n6. **Originals are sacred**; outputs get new names (`v1`, `v2`) so the user can compare. Don't overwrite a delivered file silently.\n7. **Honesty about verification**: report what you verified (visual contact sheets, QA numbers) and what you could not (e.g. couldn't listen, couldn't play on a phone).\n8. **Rights**: don't reproduce existing characters/logos/franchise art or imitate a living artist to pass as theirs; remind the user to license commercial music for public release.\n\n## 2. Workflow\n\n| # | step | what to do | tools |\n|---|------|------------|-------|\n| 1 | **Brief** | Goal, audience, platform, length, aspect, tone, assets, deadline. Fill gaps with defaults (e.g. 1080p30, 16:9, H.264). | — |\n| 2 | **Inventory** | Probe every input; note fps/VFR, rotation, HDR, missing audio, odd sizes. Proxy heavy media (4K/HEVC/ProRes). | `probe_media.py`, `make_proxy.sh` |\n| 3 | **Plan** | 6–12 line shot list: time, visual, motion, text, audio. For music: beat grid. Pick the **route** (§3). | `assets/storyboard-template.md`, `beat_detect.py` |\n| 4 | **Build draft** | Write `timeline.json`; render `--preview` (¼ pixels, seconds). | `build_timeline.py`, `autocut.py`, `ass_titles.py`, `parallax.py`, `anime_fx.py` |\n| 5 | **Review** | Contact sheet (§6) + `qa_check`. Fix issues; repeat 4–5. Show the user the draft early when feedback is plausible. | `contact_sheet.py`, `qa_check.py` |\n| 6 | **Polish** | Grade, sound design, loudness, typography, micro-timing (cuts 1–2 frames before beats). | `references/editing-craft.md` |\n| 7 | **Final render** | Full quality from originals; loudness pass inside the render. | `build_timeline.py` |\n| 8 | **QA & deliver** | `qa_check` with `--expect-*`; check first/last frame and each transition; deliver (§9). | `qa_check.py`, `contact_sheet.py` |\n\nSmall job (one trim, one conversion)? Skip the ceremony: run one ffmpeg command from `references/ffmpeg-cookbook.md`, probe the result, done.\n\n## 3. Choose the route\n\n| request | route | read first |\n|---|---|---|\n| Assemble/cut/convert footage, titles, music, grade | `build_timeline.py` (JSON timeline → ffmpeg) | `references/timeline-schema.md` |\n| Cut to the beat (AMV, montage, reel) | `beat_detect.py` → `autocut.py` → hand-tune → `build_timeline.py` | `references/editing-craft.md`, `audio.md` |\n| Animated text (word-pop captions, glitch, typewriter, karaoke) | `ass_titles.py` → timeline `subtitles` | `references/motion-design.md` |\n| Anime/manga look, impact frames, speed lines, shakes, cel look | `anime_fx.py`, `parallax.py`, speed ramps | `references/anime-production.md` |\n| Stills/key art → moving shot (motion comic, lyric-video bg) | `parallax.py` (+ Ken Burns `zoom`) | `anime-production.md` §B |\n| Data-driven / shape-heavy / templated / UI-style motion graphics | Remotion (`assets/remotion-starter`) | `references/remotion.md` |\n| Fix a broken/odd file (VFR, rotation, HDR, audio drift) | cookbook \"Normalise inputs\", `color.md` HDR | `references/ffmpeg-cookbook.md`, `troubleshooting.md` |\n| Colour grading / matching shots | timeline `grade` presets, LUTs, `eq/curves/colorbalance` | `references/color.md` |\n| Export for a platform / multiple aspect ratios | presets + `qa_check` | `references/delivery-specs.md` |\n| Generate *new* animated footage (AI image/video models) | only if a generation tool exists in this environment; else parallax/animatic | `anime-production.md` capability discovery |\n\n## 4. Tools (all in `scripts/`; every script prints `--help`)\n\n| script | purpose |\n|---|---|\n| `check_env.sh` | which binaries, encoders, filters, fonts, Python modules, GPU exist |\n| `selftest.sh` | 1-minute proof that the whole toolchain works here (synthetic media) |\n| `probe_media.py a.mp4 b.mov` | compact summary + warnings (VFR, rotation, HDR, odd size, no audio) |\n| `make_proxy.sh in.mov [dir] [h] [fps]` | light seek-friendly proxy for fast editing |\n| `contact_sheet.py v.mp4 -n 12` / `--at t1,t2` / `--range a:b` | labelled thumbnail grid PNG — **how you \"watch\"** |\n| `build_timeline.py tl.json [--preview] [--dry-run --print-graph]` | the editing engine (clips, transitions, speed ramps, Ken Burns, titles, overlays, grade, music + ducking, loudnorm) |\n| `autocut.py --music m.mp3 --dir footage/` | beat-synced starter timeline (no footage moment reused) |\n| `beat_detect.py song.mp3 [--cuts N] [--bpm X]` | BPM, beats, downbeats, onsets (numpy only; ±20 ms on steady music) |\n| `click_track.py song.mp3 beats.json --report` | numeric check that the beat grid is right (GOOD/CHECK); `-o` renders audible clicks |\n| `ass_titles.py spec.json -o t.ass` / `--demo` | kinetic typography as ASS: pop, slide, blur_in, typewriter, words, glitch, karaoke, shake_in |\n| `anime_fx.py apply in out --fx … / --preset hit --at T` | twos, boil, shake, flash, impact, chroma, glow, grain, vignette, cel, blur, punch, letterbox; `speedlines` plate generator |\n| `parallax.py scene.json` / `--layers a.png:0.1 b.png:1.0` | 2.5D camera move over depth layers, with idle bobbing |\n| `loudnorm.py in.mp4 out.mp4 [--lufs -14]` | accurate two-pass loudness for existing files |\n| `qa_check.py out.mp4 --expect-duration … --expect-size …` | decode errors, black/frozen frames, silence, A/V sync, loudness/true peak |\n\nMinimal timeline (full schema in `references/timeline-schema.md`, example in `assets/timeline.example.json`):\n```json\n{ \"output\": {\"path\": \"out/v1.mp4\", \"preset\": \"1080p30\"},\n  \"clips\": [ {\"src\": \"src/a.mp4\", \"in\": 2, \"duration\": 3, \"zoom\": {\"from\": 1, \"to\": 1.1}},\n             {\"src\": \"src/b.mp4\", \"duration\": 2, \"transition\": {\"type\": \"fade\", \"duration\": 0.4}} ],\n  \"grade\": \"cinematic\",\n  \"titles\": [ {\"text\": \"HELLO\", \"start\": 0.5, \"end\": 2.5, \"size\": 100, \"slide\": 40} ],\n  \"audio\": {\"music\": {\"src\": \"src/m.mp3\", \"volume\": 0.35, \"duck\": true}},\n  \"loudnorm\": {\"lufs\": -14} }\n```\nTotal length = Σ clip durations − Σ transition durations (transitions overlap clips). Output presets: `720p30 1080p24 1080p30 1080p60 4k30 vertical square cinema`.\n\n## 5. Recipes for the usual requests\n\n**Music montage / AMV / reel (cut on the beat)**\n1. `beat_detect.py song.mp3 --out work/beats.json`, then `click_track.py … --report` → must say GOOD, else rerun with `--bpm`/`--offset`.\n2. `autocut.py --music song.mp3 --dir src/ --beats-per-cut 2 --punch --grade anime_vivid -o timeline.json` (1 = frantic, 4 = calm).\n3. Open the JSON and hand-tune: hero shots on chorus downbeats, speed ramp into the drop, `anime_fx` hit on the biggest transient, titles/lyrics.\n4. `--preview` → contact sheet → fix → final. Cuts feel late? shift 1–2 frames earlier (`beat_detect --offset -0.05`).\n\n**Vertical social video with captions (9:16)**\n`output.preset: \"vertical\"`; `fit:\"blurpad\"` for landscape sources or reframe with `zoom.x/y`; hook in ≤ 2 s; keep text inside safe zones (top 14 %, bottom 25 % free);\ncaptions via `ass_titles.py` `words` effect (get word timings from a transcript, e.g. whisper, if available); loudness −14 LUFS.\n\n**Animated title / intro / lower third**\nSimple → `titles` in timeline (drawtext: fade, slide with ease-out). Expressive → `ass_titles.py` (pop, glitch, blur_in…). Shapes/data → Remotion starter.\nEase everything (out for entrances, in for exits), stagger 3–6 frames, hold ≥ 1 s per 3 words (`references/motion-design.md`).\n\n**Anime \"hit\" shot**\nAnticipation hold → speed ramp (`ramp`) → at contact `anime_fx.py apply in out --preset hit --at T` (ink impact frame, shake, chroma split, white flash) →\n`anime_fx.py speedlines` plate overlaid with `blend:\"screen\"` → whoosh/impact SFX. Details and variants: `references/anime-production.md`.\n\n**Stills → moving scene (motion comic / key-art animation)**\nSeparate layers (BG opaque, mid/character transparent PNG) → `parallax.py` with `dolly_in`/`pan_right`, `bob` on the character → add glow/grain/vignette via `anime_fx.py`.\n\n**Fix / convert / compress**\n`probe_media.py` → apply cookbook \"Normalise\" (CFR, even size, yuv420p, 48 kHz) → delivery preset (`delivery-specs.md`) → `qa_check.py`.\n\n**Talking-head cleanup**\nCut dead air (`silenceremove`), high-pass + denoise + gentle compression, J/L cuts with b-roll over jump cuts, captions, `loudnorm` −16 (voice) or −14 (platform).\n\n**Data/UI-style motion graphics or batch-templated videos**\nCopy `assets/remotion-starter`, `npm install`, render a still first (`npm run still`) and look at it, then render. If the browser download is blocked here, tell the user and\nprovide the project so they can render locally, or fall back to ASS + ffmpeg.\n\n## 6. Visual verification protocol (do not skip)\n\nAfter every render that matters:\n```bash\npython3 scripts/contact_sheet.py out/v1.mp4 -n 16 --cols 4 -o work/review.png          # overview\npython3 scripts/contact_sheet.py out/v1.mp4 --range 3.6:4.6 -n 8 --cols 8 -o work/t1.png   # zoom on a transition/hit\n```\nThen **open the PNG and actually look**. Check: first frame is not black/garbage · hook frame is strong · each transition looks intended (no flash/black gap) ·\ntitles are legible at thumbnail size, spelled right, inside margins · subjects aren't cropped badly · colours consistent across shots · last frame lands.\nRun `qa_check.py out/v1.mp4 --expect-duration <plan> --expect-size WxH --expect-fps N [--lufs -14]`: FAIL must be fixed; WARN must be explained or fixed\n(e.g. \"frozen 1.2 s = intentional title card\"). Compare actual duration to the plan: a mismatch usually means clamped clips or transition overlap.\n\n## 7. Taste rules (what separates pro from \"it renders\")\n\n* 80 %+ hard cuts; a transition must carry meaning. Vary shot length; accelerate into peaks, hold after them.\n* Ease all motion (out-expo for entrances, in-cubic for exits); stagger instead of animating everything together; add a rest after each reveal.\n* One grade for the whole piece; fix exposure/white balance first; match shots to a hero shot.\n* Text: high contrast, ≤ 2 typefaces, consistent positions, inside safe margins, readable at thumbnail size.\n* Sound is half the picture: duck music under speech, add SFX punctuation, fade edges, finish at −14 LUFS / −1.5 dBTP for online.\n* Effects are accents: impact frames, shakes, flashes, glitches on 5–10 % of the runtime — calm elsewhere so hits land.\n* For anime: on twos/threes, held poses with a few fluid hero beats, hard-edged shading, bloom + grain pass, SFX on every hit.\n* Hook in the first 2 s for social; end on a deliberate frame (CTA, loop, hold).\n\n## 8. Limits & failure handling\n\n* **Network**: shell downloads are often blocked; package registries usually work. If you need a file from a site, ask the user to attach it. Never retry other routes around a block.\n* **Long renders**: iterate on `--preview`; run final renders in the background if your environment supports it and poll; report by milestones.\n* **Heavy sources**: proxies for cutting, originals for the final.\n* **ffmpeg errors**: read the last error line; use `--dry-run --print-graph`; consult `references/troubleshooting.md` (symptom tables). Isolate by removing filters.\n* **Missing capability** (no fonts/libass/zscale/Remotion browser/GPU/AI generator): say what, use the fallback, mention the quality trade-off.\n* **Can't hear audio**: you can measure it (loudness, silence, peaks, beat alignment) but not judge taste; say so when audio quality is subjective.\n* **Generated/AI footage**: only if a tool exists; keep character/style references fixed, 3–6 s clips per shot, reject drifting shots; otherwise use stills + parallax and say that it's an animatic/motion-comic.\n\n## 9. Deliver\n\nSend the file (and `timeline.json` + `work/` assets if the user may iterate). Message = what it is in one sentence (length · format · look), which route you used,\nwhat you verified, any WARN you accepted, and **one** natural next step (e.g. \"want a 9:16 cut and captions?\"). Don't recap every command.\nOffer extras only if likely wanted: poster frame, vertical/square versions, `.srt`, a higher-quality master.\n\n## 10. Reference index (read when needed, not upfront)\n\n| file | read when |\n|---|---|\n| `references/timeline-schema.md` | writing/editing `timeline.json` (every field, transitions list, ramps, zoom, titles, overlays, audio) |\n| `references/ffmpeg-cookbook.md` | one-off ffmpeg operations, repairs, GIF/alpha/keying/stabilise/audio filters, encoding choices |\n| `references/editing-craft.md` | planning, pacing, structure templates, cutting rules, social/vertical, review checklist |\n| `references/motion-design.md` | easing, timing tables, choreography, kinetic type, camera/depth, ffmpeg/ASS/Remotion implementation |\n| `references/anime-production.md` | anything anime/AMV/motion-comic: pipeline, routes, shot recipes, effects, colour, sound, consistency |\n| `references/remotion.md` | programmatic motion graphics in React, audio-reactive, render options, troubleshooting |\n| `references/audio.md` | loudness targets, ducking, cleanup, beat-grid verification, SFX |\n| `references/color.md` | grading toolkit, looks, shot matching, colour-space hygiene, HDR → SDR |\n| `references/delivery-specs.md` | platform specs, safe zones, aspect conversions, export commands |\n| `references/troubleshooting.md` | any error or visual/audio/sync problem |\n"
};
export default content;
